QB.add(47,[{"id":47001,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"A pathologist reviews a biopsy from a growth. The cells are dividing without control, but every cell remains confined within its original layer, and no cells are found elsewhere in the body.<br><br>Which further feature would be needed before this growth meets the full definition of cancer?","o":["Invasion of the surrounding tissue","A higher rate of uncontrolled cell division than is already seen","Cells that still closely resemble the normal tissue of origin","A border that is well defined or encapsulated","An outward, exophytic pattern of growth away from the surface"],"e":"Cancer is defined as normal cells changing so that they grow in an uncontrolled way, invade surrounding tissue and travel to other parts of the body (metastasis). This growth already shows uncontrolled division, so what is missing is invasion (and with it, the potential to metastasise). Invasion is described as a key hallmark of cancer, and it is the feature that separates malignant from benign behaviour.","w":["Faster division alone does not make a growth malignant; invasion is the defining missing feature.","Close resemblance to normal tissue is a benign feature, not a feature of cancer.","A well-defined or encapsulated border is typical of benign tumours.","Exophytic, outward growth is the benign pattern, seen because the tumour cannot invade."],"k":"Cancer = uncontrolled growth + invasion + metastasis.","rn":[["","Cancer","Cancer","Disease caused by our cells growing in an uncontrolled way; Causing invasion of surrounding tissues; Can travel to other parts of the body"]]},{"id":47002,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"Why is cancer described as a group of diseases rather than a single disease?","o":["There are over 200 cell types, and each can give rise to its own cancer","Every cancer is caused by one single shared inherited mutation in all patients","All cancers arise from epithelial cells but in over 200 different organs","Cancers are grouped only by the patient's age at the time of the diagnosis","Each cancer is caused by a different virus acquired from the environment"],"e":"There are over 200 different types of cancer because there are over 200 different types of cells in the human body, for example lung epithelia, other epithelia and neuronal cells. Because each cell type can give rise to a different cancer, cancer is best explained as a group of diseases. This variety is one reason it is very difficult to treat.","w":["Cancer has many causes (inherited, external and natural cell process); there is no one shared mutation.","Cancers also arise from connective tissue, blood-forming cells and the lymphatic system, not only epithelium.","Cancers are classified by behaviour and tissue of origin, not by age.","Only some cancers are linked to viruses; viruses are one external factor among many."],"k":"200+ cell types → 200+ cancers → a group of diseases.","rn":[["","How many types of cancers are there Why?","How many types of cancers are there Why?","Over 200; Because there are over 200 different types of cells in the human bodies"]]},{"id":47003,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"A new antibiotic kills a bacterial infection with very few side effects, but a new anticancer drug at an effective dose damages many healthy tissues.<br><br>Which explanation best accounts for the difference?","o":["Cancer cells are only slightly abnormal compared with normal cells","Bacteria divide far more slowly than any of the body's own cells","Cancer cells lack DNA repair machinery that all bacteria possess","Cancer cells are surrounded by a capsule that blocks every drug","Bacteria are made of human cells that have been altered by the infection"],"e":"Bacteria look and work very differently from our own cells, so it is easier to hit them without harming us. Cancer cells, in contrast, are only slightly abnormal compared with normal cells. This makes it hard to achieve a good therapeutic index, meaning a treatment that hits the disease without harming the patient.","w":["The difference lies in how different the target is from our cells, not in growth speed.","The lecture does not describe cancer cells as lacking all repair; repair failure is one route to mutation.","Encapsulation is a feature of some benign tumours, not a reason for drug toxicity in cancer.","Bacteria are separate organisms that look and work very differently from human cells."],"k":"Cancer cells are only slightly abnormal, so a good therapeutic index is hard to achieve.","rn":[["","Why is cancer so hard to treat?","Why is cancer so hard to treat?","Cancer cells resemble normal cells; Poor therapeutic index"]]},{"id":47004,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"What term describes how well a treatment hits a disease without harming the patient?","a":["therapeutic index","therapeutic ratio","the therapeutic index"],"e":"The therapeutic index describes how well a treatment hits the disease without harming the patient. With bacterial infections a good therapeutic index is easier because bacteria are very different from our cells. In cancer, the cells are only slightly abnormal, so a good therapeutic index is hard to achieve.","k":"Therapeutic index = hitting the disease without harming the patient.","rn":[["","Why is cancer so hard to treat?","Why is cancer so hard to treat?","Cancer cells resemble normal cells; Poor therapeutic index"]]},{"id":47005,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"A tumour suppressor gene in a colon tumour is found to have a completely normal DNA base sequence, yet the gene is switched off because its promoter is chemically tagged.<br><br>Which type of change explains the silencing?","o":["An epigenetic change caused by promoter methylation","A point mutation that has altered one base in the promoter","A deletion that has removed the promoter region entirely","A translocation that has moved the gene to another chromosome","An inherited germline mutation passed down within the family"],"e":"Tumours arise from the accumulation of genetic alterations (mutations, deletions, translocations) and from epigenetic changes such as promoter methylation. In an epigenetic change the DNA bases are not changed directly; instead the DNA is chemically tagged (methylated), which can silence a tumour suppressor gene. Here the sequence is normal, so the change must be epigenetic.","w":["A point mutation would change the base sequence, which is normal here.","A deletion would remove DNA, but the sequence is intact.","A translocation moves pieces of DNA to another place, which has not happened.","An inherited mutation would still be a change in the DNA sequence itself."],"k":"Promoter methylation silences genes without changing the DNA bases.","rn":[["","What is an example of an epigenetic change in cancer?","What is an example of an epigenetic change in cancer?","Promoter methylation"]]},{"id":47006,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"Which tissues are most likely to acquire gene mutations through the natural cell process (stochastic events), and why?","o":["Gut epithelia and skin, because of their high rate of cell division","Neurons and lens cells, because they almost never divide again","Cartilage and bone, because of their very poor blood supply","Adipose tissue, because it stores large amounts of fat-soluble toxins","Red blood cells, because they lose their nucleus during maturation"],"e":"Many of our billions of cells copy their DNA, especially where cells are constantly removed and replaced, such as the gut epithelia and skin, which have a high rate of cell division. However perfect DNA replication is, mistakes are made. Repair machinery checks and fixes them, but sometimes the repair does not work and the result is a gene mutation.","w":["Cells that rarely divide copy their DNA rarely, so they have fewer chances for replication errors.","Blood supply is not the reason given for natural chance mutations.","Stored toxins would be an external factor, not the natural cell process.","The natural cell process depends on DNA replication during division."],"k":"Stochastic mutations arise in rapidly dividing tissues such as gut and skin.","rn":[["","Which tissues are most at risk of replication mistakes?","Which tissues are most at risk of replication mistakes?","High cell division tissues; e.g. gut epithelium, skin"]]},{"id":47007,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"Put the natural cell process that leads to a gene mutation in order. Which sequence is correct?","o":["DNA replication → mistake made → repair attempted → repair fails → mutation","Mutation → DNA replication → repair succeeds → mistake made → tumour","Repair attempted → DNA replication → mistake made → apoptosis → mutation","Mistake made → repair fails → DNA replication stops → cell dies → mutation","DNA replication → repair attempted → mistake made → repair succeeds → mutation"],"e":"Cells copy their DNA, and however perfect replication is, mistakes are made. After replication, cells check themselves for mistakes and repair them. Sometimes the repair does not work, and the result is a gene mutation.","w":["A mutation arises after replication and failed repair, not before replication.","Repair is attempted after the mistake occurs, not before replication.","Replication does not stop; the mutation persists in a living cell.","If repair succeeds there is no mutation."],"k":"Replication → error → repair → repair fails → mutation.","rn":[["","How does the natural cell process cause mutations?","How does the natural cell process cause mutations?","DNA replication mistakes; Repair machinery fails"]]},{"id":47008,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"A woman has lived all her life near a busy road, smokes, and works outdoors without sun protection. Which source of DNA change do all three exposures represent?","o":["External factors","Inherited changes passed down in families","Stochastic events from DNA replication","Epigenetic changes from promoter methylation","Natural chance errors during cell division"],"e":"DNA changes can be inherited, come from external factors, or come from the natural cell process. External factors include smoking, diet, UV irradiation, pollutants (what we breathe in) and viruses. A huge number of changes come from what we expose our bodies to.","w":["These exposures are not passed down within the family.","Stochastic events are chance replication errors, not environmental exposures.","Epigenetic change is a type of change, not one of the three sources listed.","Natural chance errors arise from DNA copying, not from smoke, traffic or sunlight."],"k":"Smoking, diet, UV, pollutants and viruses are external factors.","rn":[["","Examples of External factors that cause the change in DNA","Examples of External factors that cause the change in DNA","Smoking; Diet; UV irradiation; Pollutants; Viruses"]]},{"id":47009,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"What term means the origin or cause of a disease?","a":["aetiology","etiology"],"e":"Aetiology is the origin (cause) of a disease. Tumours arise from genetic alterations and epigenetic changes, and the DNA changes may be inherited, caused by external factors or arise through the natural cell process. These changes result in abnormal (neoplastic) cell growth.","k":"Aetiology = cause of a disease."},{"id":47010,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"A patient's report says \"benign neoplasm of the thyroid\". The patient asks whether this means they have cancer.<br><br>Which statement is correct?","o":["It is a tumour but not a cancer, because cancer means a malignant neoplasm","It is a cancer, because every neoplasm is by definition a cancer","It is not a tumour, because a tumour must always be malignant","It is a cancer, because \"neoplasm\" refers only to life-threatening growths","It is not a neoplasm, because benign growths are never new growths of abnormal cells"],"e":"Tumour and neoplasm (neoplasia) mean the same thing: a new growth of abnormal cells. A tumour can be benign (not life threatening) or malignant (life threatening). Cancer is synonymous with malignant neoplasm or malignant tumour, so all malignant tumours are cancers but not all tumours are cancers.","w":["Only malignant neoplasms are cancers.","Tumours can be benign or malignant.","Neoplasm includes benign growths.","A benign tumour is still a new growth of abnormal cells."],"k":"All cancers are tumours; not all tumours are cancers.","rn":[["","What 2 terms are synonyms of cancer?","What 2 terms are synonyms of cancer?","Malignant neoplasm; Malignant tumour"],["","Malignant Tumours","Malignant Tumours","Life threatening abnormal cell growth"]]},{"id":47011,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"Which pair of terms are exact synonyms of each other?","o":["Tumour and neoplasm","Tumour and cancer","Benign tumour and carcinoma","Neoplasm and metastasis","Malignant and borderline"],"e":"Tumour originally meant swelling but is now commonly a synonym for neoplasm, a new growth of abnormal cells. Cancer is not a synonym for tumour, because a tumour may be benign. Cancer is synonymous with malignant neoplasm and malignant tumour.","w":["Cancer refers only to malignant tumours.","A carcinoma is a malignant epithelial tumour.","Metastasis is spread to other parts of the body, not a synonym for a tumour.","Borderline tumours have some malignant features but lack invasion."],"k":"Tumour = neoplasm = neoplasia.","rn":[["","What 2 terms are synonyms of cancer?","What 2 terms are synonyms of cancer?","Malignant neoplasm; Malignant tumour"],["","Tumour","Tumour","A swelling; Now = neoplasm"]]},{"id":47012,"t":"Cancer Causes and Terminology","s":"Swollen veins surrounding visible tumours gave cancer its name. What did they resemble?","o":["The limbs of a crab","The roots of a tree","The legs of a spider","The arms of an octopus","The branches of a river"],"e":"Cancer comes from the Latin for \"crab\" or \"creeping ulcer\", and the Greek Karkinos is the giant crab of Greek mythology. Over 2000 years ago, the tumours people could see were external, for example on the breast or leg. Swollen veins surrounding these tumours resembled the limbs of a crab.","w":["Roots are not the origin of the name.","Spiders are not the origin of the name.","Octopuses are not the origin of the name.","Rivers are not the origin of the name."],"k":"Cancer = Latin \"crab\"; Greek Karkinos.","rn":[["","Why were tumours named after a crab?","Why were tumours named after a crab?","Swollen surrounding veins; Resembled crab limbs"]]},{"id":47013,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"An oncologist receives a report that classifies a tumour by behaviour but omits its tissue of origin. Why is this a problem?","o":["Both classifications are needed for accurate diagnosis, prognosis and treatment","Behaviour alone already identifies the tissue of origin in every case, so the second label adds nothing","The tissue of origin only matters for benign tumours, not for cancers","Tissue of origin can only be judged from imaging, not histology","Behavioural classification is no longer used in modern oncology"],"e":"Tumours are classified by their behaviour (benign or malignant) and their histogenesis (cell or tissue of origin). Accurate classification is essential for diagnosis, prognosis (prediction of the outcome) and treatment. If it is wrong, the oncologist will probably give the wrong treatment, wasting the patient's time and NHS money.","w":["Behaviour (benign or malignant) does not tell you which tissue the tumour grew from.","Tissue of origin matters for malignant tumours too, e.g. carcinoma versus sarcoma.","Histogenetic classification is determined histologically.","Behaviour remains one of the two current classifications."],"k":"Behaviour + histogenesis → diagnosis, prognosis, treatment.","rn":[["","Why is classification of Neoplasms important?","Why is classification of Neoplasms important?","For accurate diagnosis, prognosis and treatment"]]},{"id":47014,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"What word means a prediction of the outcome for the patient?","a":["prognosis"],"e":"Prognosis is the prediction of the outcome for the patient. Accurate tumour classification by behaviour and histogenesis is essential for accurate diagnosis, prognosis and treatment. Grading correlates strongly with survival, so it also informs prognosis.","k":"Prognosis = predicted outcome.","rn":[["","Why is classification of Neoplasms important?","Why is classification of Neoplasms important?","For accurate diagnosis, prognosis and treatment"],["","What 2 things make grading clinically useful?","What 2 things make grading clinically useful?","Predicts survival (prognosis); Guides treatment decision"]]},{"id":47015,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"A student thinks \"histogenetic classification\" means classifying tumours by their gene mutations. What does the term actually refer to?","o":["Classification by the cell or tissue of origin","Classification by the number of DNA mutations","Classification by how far the tumour has spread","Classification by how fast the tumour cells divide","Classification by the inherited genes of the patient"],"e":"Histogenetic comes from histo (tissue) and genesis (origin), not from genes. Histogenetic classification is classification by tissue or cell of origin, determined histologically. Classification by molecular and genetic features is described as the future direction, which is separate.","w":["Molecular and genetic profiling is the future classification, not histogenesis.","Extent of spread is staging.","Division rate is assessed in grading and in benign-versus-malignant behaviour.","Inherited genes are a cause of DNA change, not a classification system."],"k":"Histogenetic = histo (tissue) + genesis (origin).","rn":[["","Histogenetic classification of neoplasms","Histogenetic classification of neoplasms","cell or tissue of origin"]]},{"id":47016,"t":"Behavioural Classification","s":"Which row of the benign-versus-malignant comparison is correctly completed?","o":["Necrosis: rare in benign, common in malignant","Metastasis: rare in benign, frequent in malignant","Mitotic figures: common in benign, rare in malignant","Border: irregular in benign, encapsulated in malignant","Resemblance to normal tissue: poor in benign, good in malignant"],"e":"Benign tumours grow slowly, have rare mitotic figures, good resemblance to normal tissue, no invasion, never metastasise, have a well-defined or encapsulated border and rare necrosis. Malignant tumours grow relatively rapidly, have common mitoses, variable and often poor resemblance, invade, frequently metastasise, have poorly defined borders and common necrosis. Note that metastasis is \"never\" for benign, not \"rare\".","w":["Benign tumours never metastasise.","Mitotic figures are rare in benign and common in malignant tumours.","Benign borders are well defined or encapsulated; malignant borders are irregular.","Benign tumours resemble normal tissue well; malignant ones often poorly."],"k":"Necrosis common in malignant; benign never metastasise.","rn":[["","Why is necrosis common in malignant tumours?","Why is necrosis common in malignant tumours?","Fast growth outstrips blood supply; Centre lacks oxygen and nutrients"],["","Why are mitotic figures rare in benign tumours?","Why are mitotic figures rare in benign tumours?","Few cells dividing; Slow growth rate"],["","Is ulceration common in malignant tumours?","Is ulceration common in malignant tumours?","Yes"]]},{"id":47017,"t":"Behavioural Classification","s":"A rapidly growing malignant tumour has a core of dead cells. Which sequence best explains why?","o":["Fast growth → too few new vessels to the centre → hypoxia and lack of nutrients → central cell death","Fast growth → immune cells are recruited and destroy the centre → inflammation spreads → central cell death","Slow growth → cells reach their division limit → apoptosis → central cell death","Fast growth → capsule forms around the tumour → pressure on the centre → central cell death","Fast growth → tumour cells de-differentiate → loss of function → central cell death"],"e":"Malignant tumours grow really fast, outwards. The cells in the middle do not get enough new blood vessels (angiogenesis or vascularisation), so they cannot get enough oxygen and nutrients, and they die. The result is central necrosis, while other cells keep growing and probably breaking off.","w":["The cause given is poor blood supply, not immune attack.","Malignant tumours grow rapidly, and the death is from poor perfusion.","Encapsulation is a benign feature.","De-differentiation affects appearance, not survival of the central cells."],"k":"Central necrosis = inadequate vascular perfusion of a fast-growing tumour.","rn":[["","Why is necrosis common in malignant tumours?","Why is necrosis common in malignant tumours?","Fast growth outstrips blood supply; Centre lacks oxygen and nutrients"]]},{"id":47018,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Which group of specialists decides, by looking down the microscope, whether a tumour is benign or malignant, often without seeing the patient?","a":["pathologists","pathologist","histopathologists","histopathologist"],"e":"Pathologists often do not see the patient, but they look down the microscope and decide the classification of the tumour. They are a really important part of the cancer patient's pathway. Grading is subjective, so pathologists need a lot of training and take an annual test in their discipline.","k":"Pathologists classify tumours from histology.","rn":[["","Why do pathologists need much training to grade?","Why do pathologists need much training to grade?","Grading is subjective"],["","How are pathologists kept accurate?","How are pathologists kept accurate?","Yearly test in their discipline"]]},{"id":47019,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Under H&amp;E (haematoxylin and eosin) staining, a malignant tumour looks more like embryonic cells than its tissue of origin. What process explains this?","o":["De-differentiation, with loss of specialised features","Encapsulation by a connective tissue layer","Calcification of a central necrotic area","Exophytic growth away from the surface","Stratification of nuclei into regular layers"],"e":"Histological resemblance refers to how the tissue looks with H&amp;E staining. Benign tumours usually look very similar to the tissue of origin. Malignant cells often start expressing things that make them de-differentiate, losing their specialised features, so they look more like embryonic cells.","w":["Encapsulation is a benign feature and does not change cell appearance.","Calcification of necrosis is seen in DCIS but does not make cells look embryonic.","Exophytic growth is the benign growth direction.","Nuclear stratification is a borderline/malignant feature but is not the reason cells resemble embryonic cells."],"k":"Malignant cells de-differentiate and look more embryonic.","rn":[["","Grade 2: differentiation?","Grade 2: differentiation?","Some loss of differentiation"]]},{"id":47020,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"Which feature is part of the definition of cancer and is therefore never seen in a benign tumour?","o":["Metastasis","Pressure on adjacent tissue","Hormone production","Obstruction of a duct","Patient anxiety"],"e":"Invasion and metastasis are part of the definition of cancer, so benign tumours do neither. Benign tumours can still cause pressure on adjacent tissues, obstruction to fluid flow, hormone production, transformation into a malignant neoplasm and anxiety. Metastasis is listed as \"never\" for benign tumours.","w":["Benign tumours can press on adjacent tissue, e.g. a meningeal tumour causing epilepsy.","Benign tumours can produce hormones, e.g. a thyroid tumour causing thyrotoxicosis.","Benign epithelial tumours can block a duct.","Anxiety is a problem with benign and malignant tumours."],"k":"Benign tumours never metastasise.","rn":[["","Metastasis","Metastasis","Cancer cells spread; To other body parts"]]},{"id":47021,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"A colonoscopy finds a growth on the colon lining that rises up and outwards from an intact surface on a clearly visible stalk. Which description fits best?","o":["Pedunculated exophytic polyp, typical of a benign tumour","Sessile endophytic lesion, typical of a malignant tumour","Ulcerated endophytic tumour with an infiltrative edge","Pedunculated endophytic polyp invading the submucosa","Sessile polyp growing inwards because it cannot invade"],"e":"A benign tumour on an epithelial or mucosal surface grows away from the surface because it cannot invade. It often forms a polyp, which may be pedunculated (stalked) or sessile (sitting on the surface). This outward direction of growth creates an exophytic lesion.","w":["Sessile means no stalk, and endophytic growth is malignant.","Ulceration and an infiltrative edge are malignant features.","A stalked polyp growing outwards is exophytic, not endophytic.","Benign tumours grow outwards, not inwards."],"k":"Benign = exophytic; pedunculated (stalk) or sessile.","rn":[["","Image card: What is this colon lesion? What holds it up?","What is this colon lesion? What holds it up?","Adenomatous polyp (benign); A clearly visible stalk; So pedunculated"],["","What does a benign tumour often form on a mucosal surface?","What does a benign tumour often form on a mucosal surface?","A polyp"],["","What are the 2 types of polyp?","What are the 2 types of polyp?","Pedunculated; Sessile"]]},{"id":47022,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"What word describes a polyp that sits directly on the surface without a stalk?","a":["sessile"],"e":"A benign tumour on a mucosal surface often forms a polyp, which may be pedunculated (on a stalk) or sessile (sitting on the surface). Both grow outwards from the surface, creating an exophytic lesion. The slide diagram of a benign surface tumour shows the sessile kind.","k":"Sessile = no stalk; pedunculated = stalked.","rn":[["","Sessile","Sessile","Sitting on the surface"],["","What are the 2 types of polyp?","What are the 2 types of polyp?","Pedunculated; Sessile"]]},{"id":47023,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"Which set of features describes the diagram of a benign tumour on a surface?","o":["Intact surface, exophytic growth, homogeneous cut surface, circumscribed edge","Ulcerated surface, endophytic growth, heterogeneous cut surface, infiltrative edge","Intact surface, endophytic growth, heterogeneous cut surface, encapsulated edge","Ulcerated surface, exophytic growth, homogeneous cut surface, irregular edge","Intact surface, exophytic growth, heterogeneous cut surface, vascular permeation"],"e":"The benign diagram shows an intact surface, exophytic growth rising above the surface, a homogeneous cut surface (the inside looks the same throughout) and a circumscribed or encapsulated edge. The malignant diagram shows the opposite: an ulcerated surface, endophytic growth, a heterogeneous cut surface due to necrosis, vascular permeation and an irregular infiltrative edge.","w":["These are all features of the malignant diagram.","Benign tumours grow exophytically and have a homogeneous cut surface.","Ulceration and an irregular edge are malignant features.","A heterogeneous cut surface and vascular permeation are malignant features."],"k":"Benign surface tumour: intact, exophytic, homogeneous, circumscribed.","rn":[["","Exophytic lesion","Exophytic lesion","Grows outwards; Non-invasive outward growth"]]},{"id":47024,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"An adenomatous polyp of the colon is benign. Why is it still usually removed when found?","o":["It may be a precursor of adenocarcinoma of the large bowel","It always invades the muscularis propria within a year","It frequently metastasises to the liver while still benign","It always secretes hormones that cause thyrotoxicosis","It has already broken through its basement membrane"],"e":"The adenomatous polyp of the colon is a common lesion with a clearly visible stalk. Although benign, these lesions may be precursors of adenocarcinoma of the large bowel. For this reason they are often removed if they are identified.","w":["Invasion is not a feature of a benign polyp.","Benign tumours never metastasise.","Thyrotoxicosis is the example given for a benign thyroid tumour.","Breaching the basement membrane would mean invasion, which a benign tumour does not do."],"k":"Adenomatous polyps can transform into adenocarcinoma.","rn":[["","What can an adenomatous polyp of the colon progress to?","What can an adenomatous polyp of the colon progress to?","Adenocarcinoma of large bowel"],["","Why are colon adenomatous polyps often removed?","Why are colon adenomatous polyps often removed?","May be precursors of adenocarcinoma"]]},{"id":47025,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"Under the microscope, a colonic lesion shows cells with larger, darker nuclei that still form the glandular structure and villi of the normal colon. How would a pathologist classify it?","o":["A benign adenoma of the colon","A poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma","A borderline tumour of the colon","An invasive ductal carcinoma","A sarcoma of the colonic wall"],"e":"The slide shows an adenoma of the colon: a benign tumour of glandular epithelium. The adenoma cells have larger and darker nuclei, but otherwise they resemble normal cells and still form the glandular structure and villi. A pathologist would classify this as a benign polyp.","w":["A poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma has a solid growth pattern with little gland formation.","Borderline tumours have irregular architecture and nuclear stratification; the example given is ovarian.","Ductal carcinoma refers to the breast ducts.","A sarcoma arises from connective tissue, not glandular epithelium."],"k":"Adenoma = benign glandular tumour that keeps its glandular structure.","rn":[["","Image card: Benign colon tumour: which side is adenoma, which normal? How can you tell?","Benign colon tumour: which side is adenoma, which normal? How can you tell?","Left: adenoma; Right: normal colonic epithelium; Adenoma: larger, darker nuclei"]]},{"id":47026,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"A benign tumour of the meninges presses on the brain. Which clinical problem is given as the example of this effect?","o":["Epilepsy","Thyrotoxicosis","Intestinal obstruction","Blocked duct","Weight loss"],"e":"By definition, benign tumours are confined to their site of origin, but they may still cause problems. Pressure on adjacent tissues is shown by a benign meningeal tumour causing epilepsy. Other problems are obstruction to the flow of fluid, hormone production, transformation into malignancy and anxiety.","w":["Thyrotoxicosis is the hormone example from a benign thyroid tumour.","Intestinal obstruction is the malignant colon example.","A blocked duct is the obstruction example from a benign epithelial tumour.","Weight loss is a paraneoplastic effect of malignant tumours."],"k":"Benign meningeal tumour → pressure → epilepsy.","rn":[["","Benign meningeal tumour pressing on brain: effect?","Benign meningeal tumour pressing on brain: effect?","Epilepsy"]]},{"id":47027,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"A patient has a benign thyroid tumour that is small, confined and not pressing on anything, yet they are unwell with excess thyroid hormone. Which problem of benign tumours does this illustrate?","o":["Production of a hormone","Obstruction to the flow of fluid","Pressure on adjacent tissues","Transformation into a malignant neoplasm","Formation of secondary tumours"],"e":"Benign tumours can cause problems despite being confined, and one is production of a hormone. The example is a benign thyroid tumour causing thyrotoxicosis (too much thyroid hormone). Here the tumour is not pressing or obstructing, so the hormone effect is the explanation.","w":["Nothing is being blocked.","The tumour is not pressing on anything.","There is no evidence of malignant change.","Benign tumours never form secondary tumours."],"k":"Benign thyroid tumour → thyrotoxicosis.","rn":[["","Benign thyroid tumour producing hormone: effect?","Benign thyroid tumour producing hormone: effect?","Thyrotoxicosis"],["","What 3 other problems can benign tumours cause?","What 3 other problems can benign tumours cause?","Hormone production; Malignant transformation; Anxiety"],["","What 3 other problems do malignant tumours cause?","What 3 other problems do malignant tumours cause?","Hormone production; Paraneoplastic effects; Anxiety and pain"]]},{"id":47028,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"A small benign tumour is found incidentally. It causes no pressure, obstruction or hormone effects, and there are no features of transformation. What management approach is described for this situation?","o":["Watchful waiting","Immediate chemotherapy","Radiotherapy to the site","Sentinel node biopsy","Removal of nearby lymph nodes"],"e":"If a benign tumour is found and is not doing anything, there may be some watchful waiting. If it causes problems, it may have to be removed. Some benign tumours will eventually transform into a malignant tumour, so they may be removed for that reason.","w":["Chemotherapy is a cancer treatment and is not indicated for a quiet benign tumour.","Radiotherapy is not described for an inactive benign tumour.","Sentinel node biopsy is a breast cancer procedure.","Benign tumours do not spread to lymph nodes."],"k":"Inactive benign tumour → watchful waiting.","rn":[["","What may happen to a benign tumour causing no problems?","What may happen to a benign tumour causing no problems?","Watchful waiting"]]},{"id":47029,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"What term describes a malignant tumour's pattern of growing inwards into the underlying tissue?","a":["endophytic","endophytic growth"],"e":"Malignant tumours on epithelial or mucosal surfaces may form a protrusion early on but eventually invade the underlying tissue. This inward growth is called endophytic (\"endo\" = inwards). Benign tumours, which cannot invade, grow outwards (exophytic).","k":"Endophytic = inward (malignant); exophytic = outward (benign).","rn":[["","Exophytic vs endophytic: which is benign?","Exophytic vs endophytic: which is benign?","Exophytic (outwards)"],["","Endophytic tumour","Endophytic tumour","Grows inwards; Invades underlying tissue"]]},{"id":47030,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A surgeon cuts across a solid organ tumour and sees irregular margins with tongues of tissue penetrating the adjacent normal structures and a patchy grey core. Which interpretation is best?","o":["Malignant tumour with infiltration and central necrosis","Benign tumour with an encapsulated edge and calcification","Borderline tumour with no invasion of adjacent tissue","Benign tumour with a homogeneous cut surface","Malignant tumour confined within its basement membrane"],"e":"In solid organs, malignant tumours have irregular margins, often with tongues of neoplastic tissue penetrating adjacent normal structures. Central necrosis is often seen because of inadequate vascular perfusion. The cut surface is heterogeneous because of necrosis, with grey dead areas where the tumour is not vascularised.","w":["Benign tumours have a circumscribed edge, not penetrating tongues.","Borderline tumours lack invasion, but tongues penetrating normal tissue are invasion.","A homogeneous cut surface is benign, but this core is patchy.","Tongues of tissue penetrating normal structures are invasion, not in situ disease."],"k":"Malignant: irregular infiltrative edge + central necrosis.","rn":[["","Why do malignant tumours show central necrosis?","Why do malignant tumours show central necrosis?","Inadequate vascular perfusion"]]},{"id":47031,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Which label from the malignant surface-tumour diagram describes the tumour getting into blood vessels?","o":["Vascular permeation","Endophytic growth","Ulcerated surface","Heterogeneous cut surface","Irregular infiltrative edge"],"e":"The malignant diagram labels a heterogeneous cut surface due to necrosis, an ulcerated surface, endophytic growth, vascular permeation and an irregular infiltrative edge. Vascular permeation means the tumour is getting into blood vessels. Parts of the tumour close to a blood vessel stay alive, while poorly vascularised areas become necrotic.","w":["Endophytic growth is the inward direction of growth.","Ulceration is the broken, uneven top surface.","The heterogeneous cut surface reflects necrosis inside the tumour.","The irregular infiltrative edge is the finger-like invasion of tissue."],"k":"Vascular permeation = tumour entering blood vessels.","rn":[["","Why do malignant tumours show central necrosis?","Why do malignant tumours show central necrosis?","Inadequate vascular perfusion"]]},{"id":47032,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A breast biopsy shows ducts expanded and packed with cancer cells, a calcified central necrotic area, and an intact basement membrane. What is the diagnosis?","o":["Ductal carcinoma in situ","Invasive ductal carcinoma","Benign adenoma of the breast","Borderline tumour of the breast","Metastatic adenocarcinoma"],"e":"Normally the lumen of a breast duct is empty. In ductal carcinoma in situ (DCIS), cancer cells proliferate inside the duct, expanding it. A central necrotic area may calcify and show on a mammogram, and the basement membrane remains intact (\"in situ\" = still in its original place).","w":["Invasive ductal carcinoma has broken through the basement membrane.","An adenoma is a benign glandular tumour, not a duct filled with cancer cells.","The borderline example given is ovarian, and these cells are cancer cells.","There is no evidence of spread from another site."],"k":"DCIS = cancer filling the duct, basement membrane intact.","rn":[["","Image card: What breast cancer is this? What are A and B?","What breast cancer is this? What are A and B?","Ductal carcinoma in situ; A: calcified central necrosis; B: intact basement membrane"],["","In ductal carcinoma in situ, what fills the duct?","In ductal carcinoma in situ, what fills the duct?","Proliferating cancer cells"],["","DCIS","DCIS","Ductal carcinoma in situ"]]},{"id":47033,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"In ductal carcinoma in situ, why would the central necrotic area show up on a mammogram?","o":["It has been present long enough to calcify","It contains a high density of dividing cells","It has invaded the surrounding fatty tissue","It has a large network of chaotic vessels","It is surrounded by a thick connective tissue capsule"],"e":"In DCIS, the area labelled A is a central necrotic area. It has been there quite a while because it has calcified, so it would show on a mammogram (breast X-ray). The basement membrane, labelled B, is intact.","w":["Mitotic density is not what makes it visible on mammography.","DCIS has not invaded; the basement membrane is intact.","Vessel density is not described as the mammographic sign here.","Encapsulation is a benign feature and not what is seen here."],"k":"Calcified necrosis in DCIS shows on mammography."},{"id":47034,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Cancer cells that started in the breast ducts are seen to have broken through the basement membrane. What is the tumour now called?","a":["invasive ductal carcinoma","invasive ductal cancer","idc"],"e":"In DCIS the basement membrane is intact and the cells have not broken through the myoepithelial layer. If they break through, the tumour becomes invasive ductal carcinoma: it started in the ducts but is invading elsewhere. The treatments are different, so the pathologist's call between in situ and invasive is critical.","k":"DCIS breaching the basement membrane = invasive ductal carcinoma.","rn":[["","What is DCIS called once it breaks through?","What is DCIS called once it breaks through?","Invasive ductal carcinoma"]]},{"id":47035,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Why is a pathologist's distinction between ductal carcinoma in situ and invasive ductal carcinoma critical?","o":["The two are treated differently","Only the in situ form can calcify","Only the invasive form contains cancer cells","The in situ form is a benign tumour","The invasive form never involves the lymph nodes"],"e":"DCIS is cancer that remains within the duct with an intact basement membrane. Once it breaks through, it is invasive ductal carcinoma. The treatments are different, so the pathologist's decision between in situ and invasive is critical.","w":["Calcification is described for DCIS, but this is not why the distinction matters.","DCIS is made of cancer cells too.","DCIS is a carcinoma, so it is malignant, not benign.","Invasion raises the possibility of spread, including to lymph nodes."],"k":"In situ vs invasive changes treatment.","rn":[["","What does a tumour unlike its origin usually mean?","What does a tumour unlike its origin usually mean?","More aggressive; Treated differently"],["","Why does DCIS vs invasive matter?","Why does DCIS vs invasive matter?","Treatments differ"]]},{"id":47036,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"High-power histology of a tumour shows nuclei of many different sizes and shapes. What is this feature called?","a":["nuclear pleomorphism","pleomorphism"],"e":"Histological features of a malignant tumour include nuclear enlargement, increased mitotic activity, nuclear pleomorphism and abnormal mitoses. Nuclear pleomorphism is variation in nuclear size and shape (\"pleomorphism\" = many shapes). Abnormal mitoses do not look like a normal mitotic spindle.","k":"Pleomorphism = variation in nuclear size and shape.","rn":[["","Nuclear Pleomorphism","Nuclear Pleomorphism","Variation in nuclear size"],["","What are the histological features of A Malignant Tumour?","What are the histological features of A Malignant Tumour?","Nuclear Enlargement; Increased Mitotic Activity; Nuclear Pleomorphism"]]},{"id":47037,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Which combination of histological findings most strongly suggests a malignant tumour?","o":["Nuclear enlargement, pleomorphism and abnormal mitoses","Rare mitoses, uniform nuclei and good gland formation","Intact surface, uniform cells and a connective tissue capsule","Small dark nuclei with preserved villi and glands","Homogeneous cells with a well-defined circumscribed edge"],"e":"Malignant tumours show nuclear enlargement and increased mitotic activity, nuclear pleomorphism (variation in nuclear size) and abnormal mitoses. Benign tumours have rare mitoses, resemble the tissue of origin and have a well-defined or encapsulated border.","w":["These are benign features.","A capsule and intact surface are benign features.","Preserved glands and villi fit a benign adenoma.","Homogeneity and a circumscribed edge fit a benign tumour."],"k":"Malignant histology: big, pleomorphic nuclei and abnormal mitoses.","rn":[["","What are the histological features of A Malignant Tumour?","What are the histological features of A Malignant Tumour?","Nuclear Enlargement; Increased Mitotic Activity; Nuclear Pleomorphism"]]},{"id":47038,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A patient with an ulcerated colonic tumour becomes anaemic. Which problem of malignant tumours best explains the anaemia?","o":["Blood loss from the ulcerated surface","Production of ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone)","Obstruction of intestinal flow","Pressure on adjacent tissue","Transformation of a benign polyp"],"e":"Malignant tumours cause morbidity and mortality through pressure on and destruction of adjacent tissue, metastases, blood loss from ulcerated surfaces, obstruction of flow, hormone production, other paraneoplastic effects (weight loss and debility), and anxiety and pain. Ulceration is common in malignant tumours, and blood loss from ulcerated surfaces is a problem found only in malignant tumours.","w":["ACTH production is given for some lung tumours, not colonic bleeding.","Obstruction blocks the gut but does not explain blood loss.","Pressure damages tissue but does not explain anaemia.","Transformation is a problem of benign tumours."],"k":"Ulcerated malignant tumours bleed.","rn":[["","What 3 local problems do malignant tumours cause?","What 3 local problems do malignant tumours cause?","Pressure and destruction of tissue; Blood loss from ulcerated surfaces; Obstruction of flow"]]},{"id":47039,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Which hormones are given as examples of those produced by some lung tumours?","o":["ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) and ADH (antidiuretic hormone)","Thyroid hormone and ADH (antidiuretic hormone)","ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) and insulin","Oestrogen and thyroid hormone","Insulin and growth hormone"],"e":"Production of a hormone is a problem with both benign and malignant tumours. The malignant example is ACTH and vasopressin (ADH) from some lung tumours. The benign example is a thyroid tumour causing thyrotoxicosis.","w":["Thyroid hormone is the benign thyroid tumour example.","Insulin is not given as a lung tumour hormone.","Neither oestrogen nor thyroid hormone is given for lung tumours.","Neither insulin nor growth hormone is given."],"k":"Some lung tumours secrete ACTH and ADH.","rn":[["","ACTH","ACTH","Adrenocorticotropic hormone"],["","ADH","ADH","Antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin)"],["","Which 2 hormones can some lung tumours make?","Which 2 hormones can some lung tumours make?","ACTH; Vasopressin (ADH)"]]},{"id":47040,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A patient with advanced cancer loses a large amount of weight and becomes very weak, although the tumour is not obstructing the gut. Which explanation fits best?","o":["A paraneoplastic effect from metabolic switching as the body tries to deal with the cancer","Pressure of the tumour on adjacent tissue, which reduces appetite and food intake over many months","Blood loss from an ulcerated surface of the tumour","Production of thyroid hormone by the tumour cells","Transformation of a pre-existing benign tumour"],"e":"Other paraneoplastic effects of malignant tumours cause weight loss and debility (weakness). Paraneoplastic means effects running alongside the cancer. Clinicians often see significant weight loss because the body's metabolism starts switching as the body and immune system try to deal with the cancer.","w":["Pressure is a separate problem and is not the explanation given for weight loss.","Blood loss causes anaemia rather than this metabolic weight loss.","Thyroid hormone excess is the benign thyroid example.","Transformation describes a benign tumour becoming malignant, not weight loss."],"k":"Paraneoplastic effects → weight loss and debility.","rn":[["","Why do cancer patients lose weight?","Why do cancer patients lose weight?","Body metabolism switches; Immune system fighting cancer"],["","What 2 paraneoplastic effects are listed?","What 2 paraneoplastic effects are listed?","Weight loss; Debility"],["","Paraneoplastic effects","Paraneoplastic effects","Effects running alongside the cancer"]]},{"id":47041,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Which problem is shared by benign and malignant tumours?","o":["Obstruction of flow","Formation of metastases","Blood loss from ulcers","Paraneoplastic debility","Destruction of adjacent tissue"],"e":"Benign and malignant tumours share pressure, obstruction, hormone production and anxiety. Benign tumours obstruct fluid flow (e.g. a duct), and malignant tumours obstruct flow (e.g. intestinal obstruction). Only malignant tumours cause metastases, blood loss from ulcers, paraneoplastic weight loss and debility, pain, and destruction (not just pressure) of adjacent tissue.","w":["Benign tumours never metastasise.","Ulceration and bleeding are malignant features.","Paraneoplastic weight loss and debility are listed for malignant tumours.","Benign tumours press on tissue but do not destroy it."],"k":"Shared problems: pressure, obstruction, hormones, anxiety.","rn":[["","What 3 local problems do malignant tumours cause?","What 3 local problems do malignant tumours cause?","Pressure and destruction of tissue; Blood loss from ulcerated surfaces; Obstruction of flow"],["","Malignant colon tumour obstructing flow: result?","Malignant colon tumour obstructing flow: result?","Intestinal obstruction"],["","What 2 local effects can a benign tumour cause?","What 2 local effects can a benign tumour cause?","Pressure on adjacent tissues; Obstruction to fluid flow"]]},{"id":47042,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"An ovarian tumour shows irregular architecture, nuclear stratification, pleomorphism and quite high mitotic activity, but no invasion. How should it be classified?","o":["Borderline tumour","Benign tumour","Malignant tumour","Carcinoma in situ","Metastatic tumour"],"e":"Borderline tumours have some malignant features, such as irregular architecture, nuclear stratification, pleomorphism and mitotic activity. However, they lack the most important criterion for malignancy, invasion. Their biological behaviour is intermediate between clearly benign and overtly malignant tumours, and borderline tumours of the ovary are quite common.","w":["The malignant-looking features go beyond a benign tumour.","Without invasion it does not meet the malignant criterion.","Carcinoma in situ is the term used for DCIS, which is cancer within a duct.","There is no evidence of spread from elsewhere."],"k":"Borderline = malignant features but no invasion.","rn":[["","Which 4 malignant features do borderline tumours show?","Which 4 malignant features do borderline tumours show?","Irregular architecture; Nuclear stratification; Pleomorphism; Mitotic activity"]]},{"id":47043,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Which single criterion do borderline tumours lack that prevents them from being classified as malignant?","a":["invasion","tissue invasion","invasion of surrounding tissue"],"e":"Borderline tumours have some malignant features (irregular architecture, nuclear stratification, pleomorphism, mitotic activity) but lack the most important criterion, invasion. Their behaviour is intermediate between benign and malignant. They are treated in a specific way, different from both benign and malignant tumours.","k":"Invasion is the key criterion of malignancy.","rn":[["","Which key malignant criterion do borderline tumours lack?","Which key malignant criterion do borderline tumours lack?","Invasion"]]},{"id":47044,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Why does the borderline category matter clinically?","o":["Borderline tumours are treated differently from both benign and malignant tumours","Borderline tumours are always treated exactly as invasive cancers are, using the same regimen","Borderline tumours never need follow-up of any kind","Borderline tumours are defined by their spread to lymph nodes","Borderline tumours occur only in the colon and the breast"],"e":"Tumour classification is not always yes or no. Pathologists and medical oncologists do get cases they must call borderline, and these are treated in a specific way, differently from benign and from malignant tumours. Their behaviour is intermediate between the two.","w":["They are treated in their own specific way, not as cancers.","They have intermediate behaviour, so they are not ignored.","Borderline tumours lack invasion, so they are not defined by node spread.","The example given is borderline tumours of the ovary."],"k":"Borderline tumours have their own management.","rn":[["","Are borderline tumours treated like benign or malignant?","Are borderline tumours treated like benign or malignant?","Neither; Treated in a specific way"]]},{"id":47045,"t":"Histogenic Classification of Tumours","s":"Which definition is correct?","o":["Histopathology: the microscopic study of diseased tissue","Histogenesis: the microscopic study of biological tissue","Histology: the specific cell of origin of a tumour","Histogenetic classification: grouping tumours by their gene mutations","Histopathology: the gross anatomy of diseased organs"],"e":"Histology is the microscopic study of biological tissues, the microscopic counterpart to gross anatomy, sometimes called microanatomy. Histopathology is the microscopic study of diseased tissue. Histogenesis is the specific cell or tissue of origin of an individual tumour, and histogenetic classification is classification by tissue or cell of origin.","w":["That is the definition of histology.","That is the definition of histogenesis.","Histogenetic refers to origin (genesis), not genes.","Histopathology is microscopic, not gross."],"k":"Histology, histopathology, histogenesis: know all four definitions.","rn":[["","Histopathology","Histopathology","Microscopic study of diseased tissue"]]},{"id":47046,"t":"Histogenic Classification of Tumours","s":"What term means the specific cell or tissue of origin of an individual tumour?","a":["histogenesis"],"e":"Histogenesis is the specific cell or tissue of origin of an individual tumour (histo = tissue, genesis = origin). Histogenetic classification groups tumours by tissue or cell of origin and is determined histologically. It is one of the two current classifications, alongside behaviour.","k":"Histogenesis = tumour's tissue/cell of origin.","rn":[["","Histogenesis","Histogenesis","specific cell or tissue of origin of an individual tumour"]]},{"id":47047,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"How is the histogenetic classification of a tumour determined?","o":["Histopathological examination of stained biopsies under a microscope","Imaging the patient with X-rays and scanners to see the tumour's size and position in the body","Counting the number of involved lymph nodes at surgery","Measuring the five-year survival of similar patients","Sequencing the whole genome of the patient's blood"],"e":"Histogenetic classification uses histopathological examination: biopsies taken from patients are stained and looked at under a microscope. It specifies the tumour type by cell or tissue of origin, and the origin is built into the name, e.g. squamous cell carcinoma. Imaging and lymph node assessment are part of staging.","w":["Imaging helps staging, not identifying tissue of origin.","Lymph node counts are part of staging (N).","Survival relates to prognosis, not tissue of origin.","Genome profiling is the future molecular classification."],"k":"Histogenetic classification is made on histology.","rn":[["","How is histogenetic classification determined?","How is histogenetic classification determined?","Histopathological examination; Stained patient biopsies"]]},{"id":47048,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"At a multidisciplinary team (MDT) meeting, why are precise tumour names such as \"squamous cell carcinoma of the lung\" so valuable?","o":["They give the specialists a shared language to plan treatment together","They replace the need for the pathologist to attend the meeting and describe the slides in person","They tell the team the stage of the tumour without imaging","They show that the tumour is benign unless stated otherwise","They are used only for billing the NHS for treatment costs"],"e":"At an oncology MDT meeting the pathologist, radiologist, clinical oncologist (radiotherapy), medical oncologist (chemotherapy) and surgeons meet. Precise tumour names let them explain to each other exactly what the tumour is and decide treatment together, for example chemotherapy to shrink a tumour, then surgery, then radiotherapy. The tissue of origin is built into the name.","w":["The pathologist is one of the MDT members.","The name gives tissue of origin, not stage.","\"Carcinoma\" in a name means it is malignant.","Names are for clinical communication and treatment planning."],"k":"Tumour names are the MDT's shared language.","rn":[["","Why does tumour naming matter clinically?","Why does tumour naming matter clinically?","Shared language between clinicians; Joint treatment decisions"]]},{"id":47049,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A malignant tumour arises from the blood-forming (haematopoietic) system. What is it called?","a":["leukaemia","leukemia","a leukaemia"],"e":"The major categories of origin are epithelial cells (carcinomas), connective or mesenchymal tissues (sarcomas), the haematopoietic system (leukaemias) and the lymphatic system (lymphomas). There are many other categories and exceptions, but these are the major ones.","k":"Haematopoietic → leukaemia.","rn":[["","Haematopoietic system","Haematopoietic system","Leukaemias"]]},{"id":47050,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A malignant tumour arises in the muscle of the thigh. To which major category does it belong?","o":["Sarcoma","Carcinoma","Leukaemia","Lymphoma","Papilloma"],"e":"Connective or mesenchymal tissues include supporting tissues such as muscle, fat, bone, cartilage and vessels. Malignant tumours from these tissues are sarcomas. Carcinomas come from epithelial cells, leukaemias from the haematopoietic system and lymphomas from the lymphatic system.","w":["Carcinomas arise from epithelial (covering and lining) cells.","Leukaemias arise from the blood-forming system.","Lymphomas arise from the lymphatic system.","A papilloma is a benign tumour of non-glandular epithelium."],"k":"Mesenchymal (supporting tissue) → sarcoma.","rn":[["","Connective / mesenchymal tissues","Connective / mesenchymal tissues","Sarcomas"],["","What are malignant mesenchymal tumours called?","What are malignant mesenchymal tumours called?","Sarcomas"]]},{"id":47051,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which match of tissue of origin to malignant tumour category is correct?","o":["Lymphatic system → lymphoma","Epithelial cells → sarcoma","Haematopoietic system → lymphoma","Connective tissue → carcinoma","Mesenchymal tissue → leukaemia"],"e":"Epithelial cells give carcinomas, connective or mesenchymal tissues give sarcomas, the haematopoietic system gives leukaemias and the lymphatic system gives lymphomas. A memory aid: Carcinoma = Covering cells, Sarcoma = Supporting tissue.","w":["Epithelial malignancies are carcinomas.","Haematopoietic malignancies are leukaemias.","Connective tissue malignancies are sarcomas.","Mesenchymal malignancies are sarcomas."],"k":"Carcinoma–epithelial; sarcoma–mesenchymal; leukaemia–blood; lymphoma–lymphatic.","rn":[["","Lymphatic system","Lymphatic system","Lymphomas"]]},{"id":47052,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A report describes a \"transitional cell papilloma\". What does this name tell you?","o":["A benign tumour of non-secretory transitional epithelium","A malignant tumour of glandular transitional epithelium","A benign tumour of secretory glandular epithelium","A malignant tumour of transitional connective tissue","A borderline tumour with nuclear stratification"],"e":"Neoplasms have the suffix -oma. Benign epithelial tumours are papillomas or adenomas: a papilloma is a benign tumour of non-glandular or non-secretory epithelium, and an adenoma is a benign tumour of glandular or secretory epithelium. Squamous, transitional and basal cells are non-secretory, so their benign tumours are papillomas.","w":["There is no \"carcinoma\" in the name, so it is not malignant.","Glandular benign tumours are adenomas.","Transitional epithelium is epithelial, and the name is not malignant.","The name does not describe a borderline tumour."],"k":"Papilloma = benign non-glandular epithelium.","rn":[["","Papilloma","Papilloma","Benign tumour; Non-glandular / non-secretory epithelium"]]},{"id":47053,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"What is the name for a benign tumour of glandular or secretory epithelium?","a":["adenoma","an adenoma"],"e":"Benign epithelial tumours are papillomas (non-glandular or non-secretory epithelium) or adenomas (glandular or secretory epithelium), prefixed by the specific cell type or glandular origin. The malignant glandular equivalent is adenocarcinoma, e.g. adenocarcinoma of the breast. The benign example is thyroid adenoma.","k":"Adenoma = benign glandular tumour.","rn":[["","Adenoma","Adenoma","Benign tumour; Glandular / secretory epithelium"]]},{"id":47054,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which tumour name indicates a malignant tumour?","o":["Basal cell carcinoma","Thyroid adenoma","Squamous cell papilloma","Leiomyoma","Chondroma"],"e":"If a name contains \"carcinoma\", it is definitely cancer: for epithelial tumours, the \"carc\" part defines whether it is cancer. Papillomas and adenomas are benign. For mesenchymal tumours, the benign form is the prefix plus -oma (e.g. leiomyoma, chondroma), and the malignant form is a sarcoma.","w":["An adenoma is a benign glandular tumour.","A papilloma is a benign non-glandular epithelial tumour.","A leiomyoma is a benign smooth muscle tumour.","A chondroma is a benign cartilage tumour."],"k":"\"Carcinoma\" in a name = cancer.","rn":[["","Epithelial cells","Epithelial cells","Carcinomas"],["","Example of a tumour name showing origin?","Example of a tumour name showing origin?","Squamous cell carcinoma"]]},{"id":47055,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A malignant tumour arises from smooth muscle. What is it called?","a":["leiomyosarcoma"],"e":"Mesenchymal tumours use a prefix for the cell of origin: leiomyo- for smooth muscle and rhabdomyo- for striated muscle. Benign = prefix + -oma (leiomyoma); malignant = prefix + -sarcoma (leiomyosarcoma). Striated muscle gives rhabdomyoma and rhabdomyosarcoma.","k":"Smooth muscle: leiomyoma (benign) / leiomyosarcoma (malignant)."},{"id":47056,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which benign–malignant pair is correctly matched to its tissue?","o":["Cartilage: chondroma / chondrosarcoma","Striated muscle: leiomyoma / leiomyosarcoma","Fat: angioma / angiosarcoma","Bone: lipoma / liposarcoma","Blood vessels: osteoma / osteosarcoma"],"e":"The mesenchymal pairs are: smooth muscle leiomyoma/leiomyosarcoma; striated muscle rhabdomyoma/rhabdomyosarcoma; adipose tissue lipoma/liposarcoma; blood vessels angioma/angiosarcoma; bone osteoma/osteosarcoma; cartilage chondroma/chondrosarcoma; mesothelium benign/malignant mesothelioma; synovium synovioma/synovial sarcoma.","w":["Leiomyo- is smooth muscle; striated muscle is rhabdomyo-.","Angio- is blood vessels; fat is lipo-.","Lipo- is fat; bone is osteo-.","Osteo- is bone; blood vessels are angio-."],"k":"Lipo fat, osteo bone, chondro cartilage, angio vessels."},{"id":47057,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which tissue's tumours break the usual mesenchymal naming rule, needing \"benign\" or \"malignant\" placed in front of one shared name?","o":["Mesothelium","Synovium","Adipose tissue","Striated muscle","Cartilage"],"e":"For most mesenchymal tissues, the malignant tumour is named with -sarcoma. Mesothelium is the odd one out: it does not change to \"sarcoma\" and is just called benign or malignant mesothelioma. Synovium follows the pattern with synovioma and synovial sarcoma.","w":["Synovium gives synovioma and synovial sarcoma.","Adipose tissue gives lipoma and liposarcoma.","Striated muscle gives rhabdomyoma and rhabdomyosarcoma.","Cartilage gives chondroma and chondrosarcoma."],"k":"Mesothelioma needs \"benign\" or \"malignant\" in front."},{"id":47058,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A patient is told they have a lipoma. Which statement is correct?","o":["It is a benign tumour of adipose tissue","It is a malignant tumour of adipose tissue","It is a benign tumour of glandular epithelium","It is a malignant tumour of smooth muscle","It is a benign tumour of the blood vessels"],"e":"For mesenchymal tissues, benign tumours are named with the prefix for the cell of origin plus -oma. Lipo- denotes adipose tissue (fat), so a lipoma is a benign tumour of fat. The malignant equivalent is liposarcoma.","w":["The malignant fat tumour is a liposarcoma.","A benign glandular tumour is an adenoma.","A malignant smooth muscle tumour is a leiomyosarcoma.","A benign vessel tumour is an angioma."],"k":"Lipoma = benign fat tumour.","rn":[["","How are benign mesenchymal tumours named?","How are benign mesenchymal tumours named?","Prefix denotes cell of origin"]]},{"id":47059,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"What is the malignant tumour of glandular epithelium in the breast called?","a":["adenocarcinoma","adenocarcinoma of breast","adenocarcinoma of the breast","breast adenocarcinoma"],"e":"Glandular epithelium gives an adenoma if benign (e.g. thyroid adenoma) and an adenocarcinoma if malignant (e.g. adenocarcinoma of breast). The \"carcinoma\" part of the name shows that it is cancer. Adenomatous colon polyps may be precursors of adenocarcinoma of the large bowel.","k":"Malignant glandular tumour = adenocarcinoma.","rn":[["","Example of a malignant glandular tumour?","Example of a malignant glandular tumour?","Adenocarcinoma of breast"],["","What can an adenomatous polyp of the colon progress to?","What can an adenomatous polyp of the colon progress to?","Adenocarcinoma of large bowel"]]},{"id":47060,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which pairing of benign tumour with its malignant counterpart is NOT correct?","o":["Rhabdomyoma → leiomyosarcoma","Squamous cell papilloma → squamous cell carcinoma","Angioma → angiosarcoma","Synovioma → synovial sarcoma","Osteoma → osteosarcoma"],"e":"Rhabdomyoma is a benign striated muscle tumour, so its malignant counterpart is rhabdomyosarcoma; leiomyosarcoma is the malignant smooth muscle tumour. The other pairs are correct: squamous cell papilloma/carcinoma, angioma/angiosarcoma, synovioma/synovial sarcoma and osteoma/osteosarcoma.","w":["This pair is correct for squamous epithelium.","This pair is correct for blood vessels.","This pair is correct for synovium.","This pair is correct for bone."],"k":"Rhabdomyo = striated; leiomyo = smooth muscle."},{"id":47061,"t":"Histogenic Classification of Tumours","s":"Which statement correctly distinguishes grading from staging?","o":["Grading compares the tumour with its tissue of origin; staging describes its anatomical spread","Grading describes the anatomical spread of the tumour; staging compares the tumour with its tissue of origin","Grading decides benign versus malignant; staging decides the tissue of origin","Grading uses imaging only; staging uses histology only","Grading and staging are two names for the same assessment"],"e":"Grading describes how closely the tumour resembles its cell or tissue of origin and is determined by histological examination. Staging describes the anatomical extent of spread (\"how far has this thing spread?\") and is determined by histopathology plus clinical and radiological assessment. Students often confuse these, and also confuse them with benign versus malignant.","w":["This reverses the two definitions.","Benign/malignant is behaviour; tissue of origin is histogenetic classification.","Grading uses histology; staging uses histopathology plus clinical and radiological assessment.","They answer different questions."],"k":"Grade = resemblance to origin; stage = extent of spread.","rn":[["","Tumour Grading","Tumour Grading","Describes how closely the tumour resembles its cell or tissue of origin"],["","Staging","Staging","Anatomical extent of tumour spread"]]},{"id":47062,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"What term describes the anatomical extent of spread of a tumour?","a":["staging","stage","tumour staging","tumor staging"],"e":"Staging is the anatomical extent of spread of the tumour: \"how far has this thing spread?\" It is determined by histopathological examination plus clinical and radiological assessment, often with imaging. A memory aid: Staging = Spread.","k":"Staging = spread.","rn":[["","Staging","Staging","Anatomical extent of tumour spread"],["","Grading vs staging: which is about spread?","Grading vs staging: which is about spread?","Staging; MNEMONIC"]]},{"id":47063,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"A tumour is described as \"grade 3\". What does this tell you?","o":["It looks poorly differentiated, unlike its tissue of origin","It has spread to three separate distant organs of the body, such as the lungs and liver","It involves three or more lymph nodes in the region","It is three centimetres across at its widest point","It is a borderline tumour with three malignant features"],"e":"Grading uses numbers (1/2/3) or descriptions (low/medium/high, or well/moderately/poorly differentiated). Grade 3 is high grade and poorly differentiated: abnormal cell and nuclear shape, does not look like the tissue of origin, rapidly growing with a high mitotic index. Grade says nothing directly about spread or size, which are part of staging.","w":["Spread to organs is described by M in staging.","Lymph node involvement is N in staging.","Size is T in staging.","Borderline status is a behavioural category, not a grade."],"k":"Grade 3 = high grade = poorly differentiated.","rn":[["","Grade 3","Grade 3","High grade, poorly differentiated"],["","What 2 description-based grading scales exist?","What 2 description-based grading scales exist?","Low/medium/high grade; Well/moderately/poorly differentiated"],["","Why do malignant tumours resemble normal tissue poorly?","Why do malignant tumours resemble normal tissue poorly?","They de-differentiate; Look more like embryonic cells"]]},{"id":47064,"t":"Histogenic Classification of Tumours","s":"Which three key features are assessed when grading a tumour?","o":["Mitotic activity, cellular and nuclear features, and degree of resemblance to normal tissue","Tumour size, lymph node involvement, and distant metastases","Ulceration, necrosis, and vascular permeation","Tissue of origin, the patient's age, and family history","Hormone production, obstruction, and pressure effects"],"e":"Grading assesses mitotic activity (how many cells are undergoing mitosis), cellular and nuclear features (size and shape), and the degree of resemblance to the normal tissue (differentiation). It is determined by histological examination: the biopsy is sectioned, stained (e.g. with H&amp;E) and examined under the microscope.","w":["These are the TNM staging categories.","These are features of malignant growth, not the grading criteria.","Tissue of origin is histogenetic classification.","These are clinical problems caused by tumours."],"k":"Grading: mitoses, cell/nuclear features, differentiation.","rn":[["","What are the key features of a tumour assessed in Tumour Grading?","What are the key features of a tumour assessed in Tumour Grading?","Mitotic activity; Cellular features; Nuclear features; Size and shape; Degree of resemblance to normal tissue"]]},{"id":47065,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Why does a tumour that does not look like its tissue of origin generally need different treatment from one that does?","o":["Poor resemblance suggests it is probably more aggressive","Poor resemblance proves that it has already metastasised to distant organs","Poor resemblance means it must be benign","Poor resemblance shows its stage is low","Poor resemblance means its tissue of origin is unknown"],"e":"If a tumour does not look like its tissue of origin, it is generally probably more aggressive, and you will want to treat it differently from a lower-grade tumour. Grading correlates strongly with patient survival and changes the treatment the patient gets.","w":["Spread is measured by staging, not by resemblance.","Poor resemblance is a malignant feature.","Resemblance relates to grade, not stage.","The tissue of origin is still identified histologically."],"k":"Poorly differentiated → more aggressive."},{"id":47066,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Why do only a small proportion of tumour cells show condensed chromosomes at any one moment on a stained section?","o":["Mitosis lasts only about 30 minutes to an hour of a roughly 24-hour cycle","Most cells are destroyed by central necrosis long before they are able to reach mitosis","The staining process dissolves the chromosomes in most cells","Only cells at the tumour edge are able to undergo mitosis","Chromosomes condense only in benign tumour cells"],"e":"With staining and a special type of microscopy, condensed chromosomes can be seen using a nuclear stain. Most of the time a cell is in G1, S or G2. If the cell cycle is about 24 hours, mitosis is probably only 30 minutes to an hour, and that is when the chromosomes condense.","w":["Necrosis is not the reason given.","Staining shows the chromosomes rather than dissolving them.","No location restriction is described.","Mitotic figures are more common in malignant tumours."],"k":"Mitosis is a short part of the cell cycle."},{"id":47067,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Which protein, expressed when cells are undergoing rapid DNA replication, can be stained with an antibody to estimate proliferation?","a":["ki67","ki-67","ki 67"],"e":"Ki67 is a protein expressed when cells are undergoing rapid DNA replication. Pathologists can stain for it with an antibody against Ki67. This complements counting mitotic figures, which are visible only during the short mitotic phase.","k":"Ki67 marks rapidly replicating cells."},{"id":47068,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Which pairing of numerical grade with descriptive grade is correct?","o":["Grade 2 = medium grade = moderately differentiated","Grade 1 = high grade = poorly differentiated","Grade 3 = low grade = well differentiated","Grade 2 = high grade = well differentiated","Grade 1 = medium grade = moderately differentiated"],"e":"Grading systems differ by cancer type, but all use numbers (1/2/3) or descriptions: low/medium/high grade, or well/moderately/poorly differentiated. Grade 1 = low = well differentiated; grade 2 = medium = moderately differentiated; grade 3 = high = poorly differentiated. The grade number goes up as the tumour gets worse.","w":["Grade 1 is low grade and well differentiated.","Grade 3 is high grade and poorly differentiated.","High grade is poorly differentiated, not well differentiated.","Grade 1 is low grade, not medium."],"k":"1 low well; 2 medium moderate; 3 high poor.","rn":[["","Grade 2","Grade 2","Medium grade, moderately differentiated"],["","What 2 description-based grading scales exist?","What 2 description-based grading scales exist?","Low/medium/high grade; Well/moderately/poorly differentiated"]]},{"id":47069,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"A well-differentiated, grade 1 malignant tumour is compared with a benign tumour of the same tissue. Which statement about mitotic activity is correct?","o":["The grade 1 tumour has a low mitotic index but still more dividing cells than the benign tumour","The grade 1 tumour has fewer dividing cells than the benign tumour, because it is well differentiated","Both have identical mitotic activity because both are well differentiated","The grade 1 tumour has a high mitotic index like a grade 3 tumour","Neither tumour contains any dividing cells at all"],"e":"Grade 1 (low grade, well differentiated) tumours generally resemble their normal cells, grow slowly and have a low mitotic index. However, the lecturer notes that they still have more dividing cells than a benign tumour, in which mitotic figures are rare. Grade 3 tumours have a high mitotic index.","w":["Benign tumours have rare mitoses, fewer than a grade 1 malignant tumour.","Differentiation does not make mitotic activity identical.","A high mitotic index is a grade 3 feature.","Both contain some dividing cells; mitoses are rare in benign, not absent."],"k":"Grade 1: low mitotic index, but more than benign.","rn":[["","Grade 1: growth and mitotic index?","Grade 1: growth and mitotic index?","Slow growing; Low mitotic index"],["","Why are mitotic figures rare in benign tumours?","Why are mitotic figures rare in benign tumours?","Few cells dividing; Slow growth rate"]]},{"id":47070,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Why must pathologists take an annual test in their own discipline?","o":["Grading is subjective, and diagnosis is so critical that accuracy must stay very high","Staging systems are changed by law every year, so every pathologist must re-qualify on them","Pathologists must re-learn the TNM letters annually","Patients must consent to each pathologist every year","Grading is performed by computer and must be re-calibrated"],"e":"Grading is quite subjective, so pathologists need a lot of training. Every year they take a test in their discipline to check they still hit a very high percentage of correct diagnoses. If they don't, they can't diagnose in a hospital, because it is such a critical activity.","w":["The reason given is the subjectivity and importance of diagnosis.","The test checks diagnostic accuracy, not memory of TNM.","Patient consent is not the reason.","Grading is a subjective histological judgement by pathologists."],"k":"Grading is subjective, so pathologists are tested yearly.","rn":[["","Why do pathologists need much training to grade?","Why do pathologists need much training to grade?","Grading is subjective"]]},{"id":47071,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Why do different cancers have different grading systems?","o":["Clinicians specialise in particular cancers, and each group has its own system","Grading is decided by the patient's choice of hospital","Only some cancers can be examined under the microscope","Each grading system is based on the patient's age group","Grading systems differ by the number of lymph nodes"],"e":"Grading systems differ depending on the type of cancer. Clinicians specialise, for example in breast cancer, or in cancers of the abdominal cavity such as colorectal, pancreatic and liver, and each group has its own grading system. All of them, however, use numbers (1/2/3) or descriptions (low/medium/high, well/moderately/poorly differentiated).","w":["The choice of hospital is not the reason.","Grading is histological for all these cancers.","Age is not the basis of grading.","Lymph nodes are part of staging."],"k":"Grading systems are specialty-specific but share a 3-level scale.","rn":[["","Do grading systems differ between cancers?","Do grading systems differ between cancers?","Yes, by cancer type"],["","Is cancer one disease?","Is cancer one disease?","No; A group of diseases"]]},{"id":47072,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Two colon adenocarcinomas are compared. Tumour A still forms glandular structures similar to normal mucosa; tumour B has a solid growth pattern with little gland formation. Which interpretation is correct?","o":["A is well differentiated (probably grade 1); B is poorly differentiated (probably grade 3)","A is poorly differentiated (probably grade 3); B is well differentiated (probably grade 1)","A is benign; B is malignant","A is stage A; B is stage C in the Dukes system","Both are moderately differentiated grade 2 tumours"],"e":"A well-differentiated adenocarcinoma of the colon shows glandular structures similar to those in normal mucosa, with more dark nuclear staining than a benign tumour; the lecturer calls it probably grade 1. A poorly differentiated adenocarcinoma has a more solid growth pattern with little gland formation and \"just looks a mess\"; probably grade 3. Both are adenocarcinomas, so both are malignant.","w":["This reverses the two grades.","Both are adenocarcinomas, so both are malignant.","Gland formation relates to grade, not to Dukes stage.","They differ clearly in gland formation."],"k":"Gland formation kept = low grade; lost = high grade.","rn":[["","Poorly differentiated colon adenocarcinoma: key feature?","Poorly differentiated colon adenocarcinoma: key feature?","Solid growth pattern; Little gland formation"],["","Grade 3","Grade 3","High grade, poorly differentiated"],["","Image card: Colon adenocarcinomas: which is well vs poorly differentiated? (left vs right)","Colon adenocarcinomas: which is well vs poorly differentiated? (left vs right)","Left: well differentiated (glands); Right: poorly differentiated (solid); Arrow: gland like normal mucosa"]]},{"id":47073,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Why does grade matter to the patient as well as the pathologist?","o":["It correlates strongly with survival and changes treatment","It decides whether the tumour is a carcinoma or sarcoma","It shows whether the lymph nodes contain tumour","It tells the surgeon the tumour's exact size in millimetres","It identifies which external factor caused the cancer"],"e":"Grading is clinically useful because it correlates strongly with patient survival (prognosis) and changes what treatment the patient gets. A poorly differentiated tumour is generally more aggressive and is treated differently from a lower-grade tumour.","w":["Carcinoma versus sarcoma is histogenetic classification.","Lymph node involvement is the N in staging.","Size is the T in staging.","Grading does not identify the cause."],"k":"Grade → prognosis and treatment.","rn":[["","How strongly does grade correlate with survival?","How strongly does grade correlate with survival?","Strongly"],["","What 2 things make grading clinically useful?","What 2 things make grading clinically useful?","Predicts survival (prognosis); Guides treatment decision"]]},{"id":47074,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"Which combination of information is used to stage a tumour?","o":["Histopathology of the tumour plus clinical and radiological assessment","Histology of the tumour alone, without any imaging or clinical examination of the patient","The patient's family history and age alone","Blood tests alone, without tissue or imaging","The tumour's grade alone, converted into a stage"],"e":"Staging is determined by histopathological examination of the tumour (sections may be quite large, so invasion into surrounding tissue can be seen) and by clinical and radiological assessment of the patient, often with imaging. Radiologists are very important, and cancer care is very multidisciplinary.","w":["Grading uses histology alone; staging adds clinical and radiological assessment.","Family history and age are not staging criteria.","Blood tests alone are not described.","Grade and stage are separate measures."],"k":"Stage = histopathology + clinical + radiology.","rn":[["","What 2 things determine stage?","What 2 things determine stage?","Histopathological examination; Clinical and radiological assessment"]]},{"id":47075,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"What does the \"N\" in the TNM staging system describe?","o":["The degree of lymph node involvement","The number of mitotic figures per field","The presence of central necrosis","The nuclear pleomorphism of the cells","The number of distant organs involved"],"e":"TNM stands for Tumour, Nodes and Metastasis. T describes tumour size locally (1 small to 4 largest), N describes the degree of lymph node involvement (0 none to 2), and M describes the extent of distant metastases (0 none to 2). It is the most commonly used staging system and the basis of most others.","w":["Mitotic figures are assessed in grading.","Necrosis is a malignant feature, not N.","Pleomorphism is a grading feature.","Distant organs are described by M."],"k":"T size, N nodes, M distant metastases.","rn":[["","What does N describe?","What does N describe?","Degree of lymph node involvement"]]},{"id":47076,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"A staging sheet reads T2N1M0. Which description is correct?","o":["Medium-sized tumour, some lymph node involvement, no distant metastasis","Medium-sized tumour, no lymph node involvement, one distant metastasis","Small tumour, two involved nodes, no distant metastasis","Grade 2 tumour, one involved organ, no lymph nodes","Large tumour, some lymph node involvement, one distant organ"],"e":"T2 is a medium-sized primary tumour, N1 means some lymph node involvement, and M0 means no distant metastases. On a clinical information sheet this reads T2N1M0. M0 and N0 are what you want.","w":["N1 is node involvement and M0 is no metastasis.","T2 is medium, and N1 is some (not two) node involvement.","TNM is a stage, not a grade, and M0 means no organ involved.","T2 is medium, not large, and M0 means no distant spread."],"k":"Reading TNM: T2N1M0.","rn":[["","What does T2 N1 M0 mean?","What does T2 N1 M0 mean?","Medium-sized tumour; Some lymph node involvement; No metastasis"]]},{"id":47077,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"In TNM, which value for distant metastasis is the best outcome for the patient?","a":["m0","m 0","0"],"e":"M describes the extent of distant metastasis, shown on the slide with the lungs and liver. M0 means no metastasis, which is what we want. One organ involved is M1 and two is M2, and it can go higher.","k":"M0 = no distant metastasis.","rn":[["","What does M0 mean?","What does M0 mean?","No distant metastasis"],["","Which 2 organs illustrate distant metastasis in TNM?","Which 2 organs illustrate distant metastasis in TNM?","Lungs; Liver"]]},{"id":47078,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"Why does lymph node involvement (N1 or above) change management?","o":["It suggests cells have broken off and are travelling through the lymphatic system","It proves the tumour is benign and can safely be watched without any further treatment","It shows the tumour is well differentiated","It means the tumour has an intact basement membrane","It tells the team the tissue of origin"],"e":"N describes how many lymph nodes contain cancer. Lymph node involvement suggests metastasis: some cells have broken off and are travelling through the lymphatic system. It changes treatment; you want N0, but there is often involvement.","w":["Benign tumours do not spread to lymph nodes.","Differentiation is a grading feature.","Spread to nodes implies the tumour has invaded.","Tissue of origin comes from histogenetic classification."],"k":"Node involvement suggests lymphatic spread.","rn":[["","Why does lymph node involvement matter?","Why does lymph node involvement matter?","Suggests spread via lymphatics; Changes treatment"]]},{"id":47079,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"Why do different TNM stages matter clinically?","o":["They have different prognoses and suit different treatments","They decide whether the tumour is a carcinoma or sarcoma","They replace the need for a histological grade","They show the patient's inherited cancer risk","They are used only in breast cancer"],"e":"There are many staging systems, individualised to the organ, but TNM is the most commonly used and the basis of most. Different stages have different prognosis and are amenable to different sorts of treatment. Grade and histogenesis are still needed alongside the stage.","w":["Carcinoma versus sarcoma is histogenetic classification.","Grade and stage are both used.","Stage describes spread, not inherited risk.","TNM is used across many cancers."],"k":"Stage → prognosis and treatment.","rn":[["","Why do different stages matter?","Why do different stages matter?","Different prognosis; Different treatments"]]},{"id":47080,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"In the TNM summary figure, which statement is correct?","o":["T ranges from 1 to 4 by tumour size; N and M range from 0 to 2","T ranges from 0 to 2; N and M range from 1 to 4","T, N and M all range from 1 to 4","T ranges from 1 to 3 like grade; N and M from 0 to 1","T ranges from 0 to 4; N and M are only present or absent"],"e":"In the slide figure, T (tumour size) grows from 1 (smallest) to 4 (largest). N (lymph node involvement) runs from 0 (no nodes filled) to 1 and 2 (more nodes involved). M (distant metastasis) runs from 0 (none) to 1 and 2 (more).","w":["This reverses the scales.","N and M start at 0 in the figure.","T goes up to 4, and N and M up to 2.","N and M are graded 0, 1 and 2, not just present or absent."],"k":"T1–4, N0–2, M0–2.","rn":[["","What does T describe?","What does T describe?","Tumour size (local)"]]},{"id":47081,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"A colorectal tumour has spread through the muscularis propria but has not reached any lymph nodes. What is its Dukes stage?","a":["b","dukes b","stage b","dukes stage b"],"e":"Dukes A is a tumour confined to the submucosa; Dukes B has spread through the muscularis propria but does not yet involve the lymph nodes (e.g. pT3N0M0); Dukes C has spread to at least one lymph node (pT3N1M0); Dukes D has spread to another part of the body. Five-year survival in the figure is 90+% for A, 70% for B and 35% for C.","k":"Dukes B = through muscularis propria, nodes clear.","rn":[["","Dukes B: extent?","Dukes B: extent?","Through muscularis propria; No lymph nodes"]]},{"id":47082,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"A colorectal tumour has spread to one lymph node but there is no distant spread. What is its Dukes stage and five-year survival shown in the figure?","o":["Dukes C, 35%","Dukes B, 70%","Dukes A, 90+%","Dukes D, 35%","Dukes C, 70%"],"e":"Dukes C means the tumour has spread to at least one lymph node, e.g. pT3N1M0. In the figure the involved node turns the same colour as the tumour because it contains cancer cells. Five-year survival falls from 90+% (A) to 70% (B) to 35% (C).","w":["Dukes B has no lymph node involvement.","Dukes A is confined to the submucosa.","Dukes D means spread to another part of the body, and its survival is not shown.","Dukes C survival is 35%, not 70%."],"k":"Dukes C = ≥1 node; 35% five-year survival.","rn":[["","Dukes C: extent?","Dukes C: extent?","At least one lymph node"]]},{"id":47083,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"What pattern links the Dukes stages to five-year survival?","o":["Survival falls as the tumour penetrates deeper and spreads to nodes","Survival rises as the tumour penetrates deeper into the wall","Survival is identical at every Dukes stage","Survival depends only on the tumour grade, not the stage","Survival falls only once distant metastases appear"],"e":"In the bowel, the deeper the tumour digs and the further it spreads, the lower the chance of being alive five years later. Stage A (submucosa) has 90+% survival, stage B (through the muscularis propria, nodes clear) 70%, and stage C (at least one node) 35%. Survival already falls before distant spread, with node involvement.","w":["Deeper penetration lowers survival.","Survival differs clearly between stages.","Stage is strongly linked to survival in the figure.","Survival already falls from A to B to C, before distant metastases."],"k":"Dukes A→B→C: 90+% → 70% → 35%.","rn":[["","Why does lymph node involvement matter?","Why does lymph node involvement matter?","Suggests spread via lymphatics; Changes treatment"],["","TNM","TNM","Tumour, Nodes, Metastasis"],["","Staging","Staging","Anatomical extent of tumour spread"]]},{"id":47084,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"A colorectal tumour has spread to the liver. What is its Dukes stage?","o":["D","A","B","C","It cannot be staged by Dukes"],"e":"Dukes D means the tumour has spread to another part of the body (e.g. T3N3M1). Dukes A is confined to the submucosa, B has spread through the muscularis propria without node involvement, and C has spread to at least one lymph node. Most clinicians now use TNM, but Dukes may still be heard.","w":["A is confined to the submucosa.","B has no distant or nodal spread.","C has nodal spread but no distant spread.","Distant spread is Dukes D."],"k":"Dukes D = distant spread."},{"id":47085,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"Which staging system is the traditional system for colorectal cancer, with stages A to D?","a":["dukes","dukes classification","dukes staging","dukes'","dukes system"],"e":"The Dukes classification is the traditional staging system for colorectal cancer, with stages A, B, C and D. Most clinicians have now converted to TNM, but you may still hear Dukes from people treating colorectal cancer, probably depending on how long they have been in the field.","k":"Dukes = traditional colorectal staging.","rn":[["","Which cancer uses Dukes classification?","Which cancer uses Dukes classification?","Colorectal cancer"],["","What are the Dukes stages?","What are the Dukes stages?","A, B, C, D"]]},{"id":47086,"t":"Classifications Now and Future","s":"What is described as the future of tumour classification?","o":["Classification by molecular and genetic features for targeted, individualised management","Classification by the gross appearance of the tumour alone, as seen by the surgeon at operation","Classification by the patient's age and sex alone","Abandoning grading and staging altogether","Classifying all tumours as either benign or malignant only"],"e":"Current classification is behavioural (benign/malignant, grade, stage) and histogenetic (tissue or cell of origin). The future is classification by molecular and genetic features, for example a whole-genome profile of how many and what types of mutations a tumour has. This allows targeted, individualised management with therapies aimed at proteins deregulated in the tumour.","w":["Gross appearance alone is not the future direction described.","Age and sex are not the basis described.","Grading and staging remain current classifications.","Behaviour is only one part of current classification."],"k":"Future: molecular/genetic classification → targeted therapy.","rn":[["","What management do molecular features allow?","What management do molecular features allow?","Targeted individualised management"],["","What is the future basis of classification?","What is the future basis of classification?","Molecular and genetic features"]]},{"id":47087,"t":"Classifications Now and Future","s":"A patient's tumour is stained for a protein known to be deregulated in that tumour type, so that a matching drug can be chosen. What approach does this represent?","o":["Targeted, individualised management","Histogenetic classification","Behavioural classification","TNM staging","Watchful waiting"],"e":"There are now many targeted therapies aimed at proteins that are deregulated in the tumour, so tumours will be stained for those. Treatment can be tailored to the patient's tumour and to how well the patient is. This is part of the future of classification by molecular and genetic features.","w":["Histogenetic classification identifies the tissue of origin.","Behavioural classification decides benign or malignant, grade and stage.","TNM describes extent of spread.","Watchful waiting is for inactive benign tumours."],"k":"Targeted therapy aims at deregulated tumour proteins.","rn":[["","What management do molecular features allow?","What management do molecular features allow?","Targeted individualised management"]]},{"id":47088,"t":"Classifications Now and Future","s":"A patient has a cancer deposit in the liver, but no original site can be found. Why are such patients described as really hard to treat?","o":["The tissue of origin is unknown, which classification needs for treatment","They always have benign tumours that cannot be removed safely because of their position","They have a grade that cannot be measured histologically","They have tumours that never spread to lymph nodes","They have tumours that cannot be biopsied at all"],"e":"Sometimes clinicians find cancer but cannot tell the original site of origin; they think what they found is a metastasis. These patients are really hard to treat. The original tumour may have been very small and broken off very early. Classification by tissue of origin is essential for correct treatment.","w":["A metastasis means the cancer is malignant.","Grading can still be done on a biopsy.","Node involvement is not the reason given.","The deposit can be biopsied; the issue is the unknown origin."],"k":"Unknown primary: no tissue of origin → hard to treat.","rn":[["","Why is an unknown primary site hard to treat?","Why is an unknown primary site hard to treat?","Origin unknown, only metastasis found"]]},{"id":47089,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"In breast cancer surgery, which node is biopsied as the main node closest to the breast, using a blue dye?","a":["sentinel node","sentinel lymph node","the sentinel node"],"e":"In breast cancer surgery a sentinel node biopsy is done on the main node closest to the breast. A blue dye is injected and, if cancer cells are there, it seems to stain them, so nodes that go blue contain cancer. This is specific to breast cancer and was noted as not being for the AKT.","k":"Sentinel node = main node closest to the breast.","rn":[["","TNM","TNM","Tumour, Nodes, Metastasis"],["","Dukes C: extent?","Dukes C: extent?","At least one lymph node"]]},{"id":47090,"t":"Classifications Now and Future","s":"For each question, which classification answers it? \"Does it resemble its tissue? Where has it spread? Where did it originate?\"","o":["Grade; stage; histogenetic","Stage; grade; histogenetic","Histogenetic; stage; grade","Grade; histogenetic; stage","Behaviour; grade; stage"],"e":"Grade asks whether the tumour resembles its tissue of origin. Stage asks where it has spread: still in its tissue of origin or somewhere else. Histogenetic classification asks where it originated. Behavioural classification (benign or malignant) asks how it behaves.","w":["Grade and stage are reversed.","Resemblance is grade, origin is histogenetic.","Spread is stage, and origin is histogenetic.","Behaviour asks benign or malignant."],"k":"Grade = resemblance; stage = spread; histogenetic = origin.","rn":[["","What 3 behavioural questions classify a tumour today?","What 3 behavioural questions classify a tumour today?","Benign or malignant?; Grade?; Stage?"]]},{"id":47091,"t":"Behavioural Classification","s":"A tumour removed from the abdomen is slow-growing, has a connective tissue capsule, and its cells closely resemble the surrounding tissue. Which further finding would be MOST surprising?","o":["A deposit of identical cells in the liver","Rare mitotic figures on histology","Little or no necrosis","A homogeneous cut surface","Pressure on a nearby organ"],"e":"Slow growth, encapsulation and good resemblance to normal tissue are benign features. Benign tumours have rare mitoses, rare necrosis and a homogeneous cut surface, and they can press on nearby tissue. They never metastasise, so a deposit in the liver would contradict a benign diagnosis.","w":["Rare mitoses fit a benign tumour.","Rare necrosis fits a benign tumour.","A homogeneous cut surface fits a benign tumour.","Benign tumours can cause pressure on adjacent tissue."],"k":"Metastasis is never a benign feature."},{"id":47092,"t":"Behavioural Classification","s":"Which statement about malignant tumours is NOT correct?","o":["They always lack mitotic figures","They often show necrosis","Their resemblance to normal tissue is variable","They frequently metastasise","Their borders are poorly defined or irregular"],"e":"Malignant tumours grow relatively rapidly with common mitotic figures, variable and often poor histological resemblance, tissue invasion, frequent metastasis, poorly defined or irregular borders and common necrosis. Rare mitotic figures are a benign feature, so \"always lack mitotic figures\" is wrong.","w":["Necrosis is common in malignant tumours.","Resemblance is variable, often poor.","Metastasis is frequent, especially if diagnosed late.","Malignant borders are irregular."],"k":"Mitotic figures are common in malignant tumours.","rn":[["","Why are mitotic figures rare in benign tumours?","Why are mitotic figures rare in benign tumours?","Few cells dividing; Slow growth rate"],["","What are mitotic figures?","What are mitotic figures?","Dividing cells; Condensed chromosomes under microscope"]]},{"id":47093,"t":"Behavioural Classification","s":"What are \"mitotic figures\" seen down the microscope?","o":["Cells undergoing mitosis with condensed chromosomes","Dead cells in the centre of a tumour","Nuclei stacked in layers in the epithelium","Cells that have de-differentiated to look embryonic","Tumour cells inside a blood vessel"],"e":"Mitotic figures are cells seen down the microscope undergoing mitosis (cell division), with their chromosomes condensed. They are rare in benign tumours, because few cells are going round the cell cycle, which fits slow growth. In malignant tumours you see many cells with condensed chromosomes.","w":["Dead central cells are necrosis.","Nuclei stacked in layers is nuclear stratification.","This describes de-differentiation.","This describes vascular permeation."],"k":"Mitotic figures = dividing cells with condensed chromosomes.","rn":[["","What are mitotic figures?","What are mitotic figures?","Dividing cells; Condensed chromosomes under microscope"]]},{"id":47094,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"What word describes the outward, non-invasive direction of growth of a benign tumour on a surface?","a":["exophytic","exophytic growth","exophytic lesion"],"e":"A benign tumour on an epithelial or mucosal surface grows away from the surface, up and outwards, because it cannot invade. This creates an exophytic lesion (\"exo\" = outwards), often a pedunculated or sessile polyp. Malignant tumours grow endophytically (inwards).","k":"Exophytic = outward (benign).","rn":[["","Exophytic lesion","Exophytic lesion","Grows outwards; Non-invasive outward growth"],["","Exophytic vs endophytic: which is benign?","Exophytic vs endophytic: which is benign?","Exophytic (outwards)"]]},{"id":47095,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"What feature, meaning nuclei stacked in layers, is seen in borderline tumours?","a":["nuclear stratification","stratification"],"e":"Borderline tumours show some malignant features: irregular architecture, nuclear stratification (nuclei stacked in layers), pleomorphism and mitotic activity. They lack invasion, so their behaviour is intermediate. Borderline tumours of the ovary are quite common.","k":"Nuclear stratification = nuclei stacked in layers.","rn":[["","Which 4 malignant features do borderline tumours show?","Which 4 malignant features do borderline tumours show?","Irregular architecture; Nuclear stratification; Pleomorphism; Mitotic activity"]]},{"id":47096,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"Which problem caused by tumours is listed ONLY for malignant tumours?","o":["Pain","Anxiety","Pressure","Obstruction","Hormone production"],"e":"Malignant tumours cause pressure and destruction of tissue, metastases, blood loss from ulcers, obstruction, hormone production, paraneoplastic weight loss and debility, and anxiety and pain. Benign tumours cause pressure, obstruction, hormone production, transformation and anxiety. Pain, blood loss and paraneoplastic effects are listed only for malignant tumours.","w":["Anxiety occurs with both.","Pressure occurs with both.","Obstruction occurs with both.","Hormone production occurs with both."],"k":"Pain, bleeding and paraneoplastic effects are malignant-only.","rn":[["","What 3 other problems do malignant tumours cause?","What 3 other problems do malignant tumours cause?","Hormone production; Paraneoplastic effects; Anxiety and pain"]]},{"id":47097,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"A patient with a harmless benign skin lump is very distressed, fearing it is something more sinister. Which problem of benign tumours is this?","o":["Anxiety","Pressure","Obstruction","Hormone production","Transformation"],"e":"Benign tumours are confined to their site of origin but can cause pressure, obstruction, hormone production, transformation into malignancy and anxiety. Anxiety means the patient fears the lesion may be something more sinister.","w":["There is no pressure effect described.","Nothing is being blocked.","No hormone is being produced.","There is no malignant change described."],"k":"Anxiety is a real problem even with benign tumours.","rn":[["","Why do benign tumours cause anxiety?","Why do benign tumours cause anxiety?","Fear of something more sinister"],["","What 3 other problems can benign tumours cause?","What 3 other problems can benign tumours cause?","Hormone production; Malignant transformation; Anxiety"]]},{"id":47098,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"Why is ulceration common in malignant surface tumours but not in benign ones?","o":["Malignant tumours break down the surface as they grow and invade, while benign tumours keep it intact","Benign tumours always have a thicker and better organised blood supply that protects and nourishes the surface layer","Malignant tumours grow outwards on stalks that twist and ulcerate","Benign tumours secrete hormones that heal the surface layer","Malignant tumours are encapsulated, which pressure-ulcerates the surface"],"e":"The benign diagram shows an intact surface over an exophytic tumour. Malignant tumours may form a protrusion early but invade the underlying tissue, growing endophytically; ulceration is common, and the surface breaks down and becomes uneven. Blood loss from ulcerated surfaces is a problem of malignant tumours.","w":["Blood supply is not the explanation given.","Stalked, outward polyps are benign.","Hormone production is unrelated to surface integrity.","Encapsulation is a benign feature."],"k":"Malignant: ulcerated surface; benign: intact surface.","rn":[["","Why does a benign tumour grow away from the surface?","Why does a benign tumour grow away from the surface?","It cannot invade"]]},{"id":47099,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A tumour arises from the lining cells of the bronchus and invades the lung. Which name category applies?","o":["Carcinoma","Sarcoma","Lymphoma","Leukaemia","Papilloma"],"e":"Epithelial cells (lining and covering cells) give rise to carcinomas when malignant. An invasive tumour of bronchial lining cells is therefore a carcinoma, e.g. squamous cell carcinoma of the lung. A papilloma would be a benign, non-invasive tumour of non-glandular epithelium.","w":["Sarcomas arise from connective tissue.","Lymphomas arise from the lymphatic system.","Leukaemias arise from the haematopoietic system.","An invasive tumour is malignant, and papillomas are benign."],"k":"Malignant lining-cell tumour = carcinoma.","rn":[["","Epithelial cells","Epithelial cells","Carcinomas"],["","Example of a tumour name showing origin?","Example of a tumour name showing origin?","Squamous cell carcinoma"],["","What are malignant epithelial tumours called?","What are malignant epithelial tumours called?","Carcinomas"]]},{"id":47100,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"What is the malignant tumour of bone called?","a":["osteosarcoma"],"e":"Bone gives osteoma (benign) and osteosarcoma (malignant). Malignant mesenchymal tumours are sarcomas, with the prefix denoting the cell of origin: osteo- for bone, chondro- for cartilage, lipo- for fat, angio- for blood vessels.","k":"Bone: osteoma / osteosarcoma."},{"id":47101,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"What is the benign tumour of striated muscle called?","a":["rhabdomyoma"],"e":"Striated muscle gives rhabdomyoma (benign) and rhabdomyosarcoma (malignant). Smooth muscle gives leiomyoma and leiomyosarcoma. The prefix denotes the cell of origin.","k":"Striated muscle: rhabdomyoma / rhabdomyosarcoma."},{"id":47102,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which tumour name describes a malignant tumour of blood vessels?","o":["Angiosarcoma","Angioma","Leiomyoma","Synovioma","Chondroma"],"e":"Blood vessels give angioma (benign) and angiosarcoma (malignant). Leiomyoma is a benign smooth muscle tumour, synovioma is a benign synovial tumour and chondroma is a benign cartilage tumour.","w":["Angioma is the benign blood vessel tumour.","Leiomyoma is a benign smooth muscle tumour.","Synovioma is the benign synovial tumour.","Chondroma is the benign cartilage tumour."],"k":"Blood vessels: angioma / angiosarcoma."},{"id":47103,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which statement about naming epithelial versus mesenchymal tumours is correct?","o":["Malignant epithelial tumours are carcinomas; malignant mesenchymal tumours are sarcomas","Malignant epithelial tumours are sarcomas; malignant mesenchymal tumours are carcinomas","All benign tumours are called papillomas whatever their tissue","Benign mesenchymal tumours are always named adenomas","Mesenchymal tumours are always named with \"benign\" or \"malignant\" in front"],"e":"Benign epithelial tumours are papillomas or adenomas, and malignant epithelial tumours are carcinomas. For connective or mesenchymal tissue, the benign tumour is named with a prefix for the cell of origin plus -oma, and the malignant tumour is a sarcoma. Mesothelioma is the exception needing \"benign\" or \"malignant\" in front.","w":["This reverses the two categories.","Papillomas are benign non-glandular epithelial tumours only.","Adenomas are benign glandular epithelial tumours.","Only mesothelioma uses this pattern."],"k":"Epithelial → carcinoma; mesenchymal → sarcoma.","rn":[["","What are malignant mesenchymal tumours called?","What are malignant mesenchymal tumours called?","Sarcomas"],["","What are malignant epithelial tumours called?","What are malignant epithelial tumours called?","Carcinomas"]]},{"id":47104,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"Which statement about the causes of tumours is correct?","o":["Both genetic alterations and epigenetic changes can produce neoplastic growth","Only inherited mutations are able to cause cancer","Epigenetic changes alter the order of DNA bases","External factors cause cancer without changing DNA","Tumours arise from a single mutation present at birth"],"e":"Tumours arise from the accumulation of genetic alterations (mutations, deletions, translocations) and from epigenetic changes (promoter methylation). DNA changes can be inherited, come from external factors (smoking, diet, UV, pollutants, viruses), or arise from the natural cell process. These changes result in abnormal neoplastic cell growth.","w":["External factors and natural cell processes also cause DNA changes.","Epigenetic changes do not change the DNA bases directly.","External factors cause changes to DNA.","Genetic alterations accumulate over time."],"k":"Genetic + epigenetic changes → neoplasia.","rn":[["","Cause of Cancer","Cause of Cancer","Genetic Alterations; Epigenetic Changes in cells"]]},{"id":47105,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"Which change type is an example of a genetic alteration in which a piece of DNA is moved to another place?","a":["translocation","translocations","chromosomal translocation"],"e":"Genetic alterations include mutations (e.g. point mutations), deletions (missing pieces) and translocations (pieces moved to another place). They build up over time. Epigenetic changes such as promoter methylation are a separate category that does not change the DNA bases.","k":"Translocation = DNA moved to another place.","rn":[["","Examples of Genetic Alterations","Examples of Genetic Alterations","Mutations; Deletions; Translocations"]]},{"id":47106,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"A student writes: \"A high-grade tumour has spread widely.\" Why is this statement unsafe?","o":["Grade describes appearance, not spread, which is staging","Grade describes spread, but only to lymph nodes","High-grade tumours are by definition benign","Grade is determined by imaging, so it cannot judge spread","Grade describes tissue of origin, not spread"],"e":"Grading describes how closely the tumour resembles its cell or tissue of origin: a high-grade tumour is poorly differentiated and probably more aggressive. Staging describes the anatomical extent of spread. A high-grade tumour has not necessarily spread; that is assessed separately by staging. Students often confuse grading and staging.","w":["Lymph node spread is N in staging.","Grade applies to malignant tumours and says nothing about benignity.","Grade is determined histologically.","Tissue of origin is histogenetic classification."],"k":"Grade ≠ stage.","rn":[["","Grading vs staging: which is about spread?","Grading vs staging: which is about spread?","Staging; MNEMONIC"],["","Staging","Staging","Anatomical extent of tumour spread"]]},{"id":47107,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"Which TNM code describes a large primary tumour with no lymph node involvement and no distant metastasis?","o":["T4N0M0","T1N2M0","T4N2M2","T1N0M0","T2N1M1"],"e":"T describes tumour size from 1 (small) to 4 (largest). N0 means no lymph node involvement and M0 means no distant metastasis. A large tumour without node or distant spread is therefore T4N0M0.","w":["T1 is small, and N2 means node involvement.","N2 and M2 mean node and distant spread.","T1 is a small tumour.","N1 and M1 mean node and distant spread."],"k":"T = size; N0 M0 = no spread."},{"id":47108,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"What is the Dukes stage of a colorectal tumour confined to the submucosa?","a":["a","dukes a","stage a","dukes stage a"],"e":"Dukes A is a tumour confined to the submucosa (e.g. pT1N0M0), with five-year survival of 90+% in the figure. The tumour stays in the shallow layers with no node involvement or metastasis.","k":"Dukes A = submucosa; 90+% five-year survival.","rn":[["","Dukes A: extent?","Dukes A: extent?","Confined to submucosa"]]},{"id":47109,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"In the Dukes figure, what happens to the appearance of a lymph node once it contains tumour?","o":["It becomes the same colour as the tumour","It disappears from the figure","It turns grey like a normal node","It shrinks and calcifies","It turns blue after dye injection"],"e":"In the Dukes figure, a lymph node without tumour is shown grey. In stage C, the involved node changes to the same purple as the tumour because it contains cancer cells. Blue dye staining belongs to breast sentinel node biopsy, which is a different procedure.","w":["The node is still shown.","Grey indicates a node without tumour.","Calcification is described in DCIS.","Blue dye is the breast sentinel node technique."],"k":"Involved node = same colour as tumour (Dukes C)."},{"id":47110,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"Which pair are the two current ways of classifying tumours?","o":["Behavioural and histogenetic","Grading and histogenetic","Staging and molecular","Molecular and genetic","Behavioural and molecular"],"e":"Tumours are currently classified by behaviour (benign or malignant, with grade and stage describing behaviour) and by histogenesis (cell or tissue of origin, determined histologically). Classification by molecular and genetic features is described as the future.","w":["Grading is part of describing behaviour.","Molecular classification is the future.","Molecular and genetic features are the future approach.","Molecular classification is the future, not current."],"k":"Now: behavioural + histogenetic.","rn":[["","What are tumours classified upon?","What are tumours classified upon?","Behavioural classification; Histogenetic classification"]]},{"id":47111,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"Which statement about the problems caused by benign tumours is correct?","o":["They can cause disease despite being confined to their site of origin","They never cause any clinical problem because they cannot invade","They always need urgent chemotherapy","They cause problems only by metastasising","They cause problems only if they ulcerate"],"e":"By definition benign tumours are confined to their site of origin, but they may still cause clinical problems: pressure on adjacent tissues, obstruction to fluid flow, hormone production, transformation into a malignant neoplasm and anxiety. There may be watchful waiting, or removal if they cause problems.","w":["Benign tumours can still cause clinical problems.","Chemotherapy is not described for benign tumours.","Benign tumours never metastasise.","Ulceration is a malignant feature."],"k":"Benign but still harmful: pressure, obstruction, hormones, transformation, anxiety.","rn":[["","Where are benign tumours confined, by definition?","Where are benign tumours confined, by definition?","Their site of origin"]]},{"id":47112,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"What term describes how many cells in a tumour are dividing, which is low in grade 1 and high in grade 3?","a":["mitotic index","the mitotic index"],"e":"The mitotic index is how many cells are dividing. Grade 1 tumours grow slowly and have a low mitotic index; grade 2 tumours grow more rapidly; grade 3 tumours grow rapidly with a high mitotic index.","k":"Mitotic index = proportion of dividing cells.","rn":[["","Grade 3: growth and mitotic index?","Grade 3: growth and mitotic index?","Rapidly growing; High mitotic index"],["","Grade 1: growth and mitotic index?","Grade 1: growth and mitotic index?","Slow growing; Low mitotic index"]]},{"id":47113,"t":"Behavioural Classification","s":"Why do parts of a malignant tumour close to a blood vessel stay alive while other areas become necrotic?","o":["They receive enough oxygen and nutrients from the vessel","They are protected by a connective tissue capsule","They divide too slowly to need any oxygen or nutrients from the blood","They are benign cells mixed into the tumour","They have stopped invading the nearby tissue"],"e":"Central necrosis happens because of inadequate vascular perfusion: cells that are not vascularised cannot get enough oxygen and nutrients and die. Parts close to a blood vessel stay alive because they are supplied. This produces the heterogeneous cut surface of a malignant tumour.","w":["Encapsulation is a benign feature.","Malignant cells divide rapidly.","They are malignant cells, not benign ones.","Survival depends on blood supply, not on invasion."],"k":"Perfusion decides which tumour cells survive.","rn":[["","Why is necrosis common in malignant tumours?","Why is necrosis common in malignant tumours?","Fast growth outstrips blood supply; Centre lacks oxygen and nutrients"]]},{"id":47114,"t":"Cancer Classification","s":"Which pair of features defines cancer and separates malignant tumours from benign ones?","o":["Invasion and metastasis","Pressure and obstruction","Necrosis and calcification","Hormones and anxiety","Polyp formation and a stalk"],"e":"Cancer is defined by uncontrolled growth, invasion of surrounding tissue and travel to other parts of the body (metastasis). Benign tumours neither invade nor metastasise. Pressure, obstruction, hormones and anxiety can occur with both, and stalked polyps are benign.","w":["These occur with both benign and malignant tumours.","Necrosis is commoner in malignant tumours but is not the defining feature.","These occur with both.","Stalked polyps are benign."],"k":"Invasion + metastasis define malignancy.","rn":[["","Cancer","Cancer","Disease caused by our cells growing in an uncontrolled way; Causing invasion of surrounding tissues; Can travel to other parts of the body"],["","Metastasis","Metastasis","Cancer cells spread; To other body parts"]]},{"id":47115,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Which tumour is likely to have the worst prognosis based on grade alone?","o":["A poorly differentiated tumour with a high mitotic index","A well-differentiated tumour with normal tissue organisation","A tumour that generally resembles its normal cells","A slowly growing tumour with a low mitotic index","A moderately differentiated tumour with some loss of differentiation"],"e":"Grading correlates strongly with patient survival. Grade 3 (high grade, poorly differentiated) tumours have abnormal cell and nuclear shape, do not look like the tissue of origin and grow rapidly with a high mitotic index. These are generally more aggressive than grade 1 or 2 tumours.","w":["This is grade 1, with a better prognosis.","This describes grade 1.","This describes grade 1.","This is grade 2, better than grade 3."],"k":"Poorly differentiated = high grade = worse prognosis.","rn":[["","Grade 3","Grade 3","High grade, poorly differentiated"],["","Grade 3: growth and mitotic index?","Grade 3: growth and mitotic index?","Rapidly growing; High mitotic index"]]},{"id":47116,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"Why are radiologists described as very important in cancer staging?","o":["Imaging contributes to the clinical and radiological assessment of spread","They decide the tumour's grade from its microscopic appearance on stained sections","They perform the histological staining of biopsies","They classify the tumour's tissue of origin","They give chemotherapy to shrink the tumour"],"e":"Staging is determined by histopathological examination plus clinical and radiological assessment of the patient, often involving imaging such as scanners and chest X-rays. This makes radiologists very important, and diagnosing and treating cancer is very multidisciplinary.","w":["Grade is decided by pathologists on histology.","Staining is part of histopathology.","Tissue of origin is a histological judgement.","Chemotherapy is given by medical oncologists."],"k":"Imaging is central to staging.","rn":[["","What does radiological assessment often involve?","What does radiological assessment often involve?","Imaging techniques"],["","What 2 things determine stage?","What 2 things determine stage?","Histopathological examination; Clinical and radiological assessment"]]},{"id":47117,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"Which MDT member gives radiotherapy?","o":["Clinical oncologist","Medical oncologist","Pathologist","Radiologist","Surgeon"],"e":"At an oncology MDT meeting: the pathologist reads the tissue, the radiologist interprets imaging, the clinical oncologist gives radiotherapy, the medical oncologist gives chemotherapy and the surgeons operate. They use precise tumour names to plan treatment together.","w":["The medical oncologist gives chemotherapy.","The pathologist examines tissue under the microscope.","The radiologist interprets imaging.","The surgeon operates."],"k":"Clinical oncologist = radiotherapy; medical oncologist = chemotherapy.","rn":[["","What does a clinical oncologist give?","What does a clinical oncologist give?","Radiotherapy"],["","Which 2 oncologists attend a cancer MDT?","Which 2 oncologists attend a cancer MDT?","Clinical oncologist; Medical oncologist"],["","What does a medical oncologist give?","What does a medical oncologist give?","Chemotherapy"]]},{"id":47118,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"In ductal carcinoma in situ, which structure labelled B on the slide is still intact?","a":["basement membrane","the basement membrane"],"e":"In DCIS, A is a calcified central necrotic area and B is the basement membrane, which is intact. The lecturer adds that the cells have not broken through the myoepithelial layer. If they break through, the tumour becomes invasive ductal carcinoma.","k":"DCIS: basement membrane intact.","rn":[["","Why is DCIS \"in situ\"?","Why is DCIS \"in situ\"?","Basement membrane intact; Not through myoepithelial layer"],["","Image card: What breast cancer is this? What are A and B?","What breast cancer is this? What are A and B?","Ductal carcinoma in situ; A: calcified central necrosis; B: intact basement membrane"]]},{"id":47119,"t":"Histogenic Classification of Tumours","s":"What is the microscopic counterpart to gross anatomy, sometimes called microanatomy?","a":["histology"],"e":"Histology is the microscopic study of biological tissues, the microscopic counterpart to gross anatomy, and is sometimes called microanatomy. Histopathology is the microscopic study of diseased tissue.","k":"Histology = microanatomy.","rn":[["","Histology","Histology","Microscopic study of biological tissues.; It is the microscopic counterpart to gross anatomy; Can be referred to as micro-anatomy"]]},{"id":47120,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A patient has a malignant tumour of the joint lining (synovium). What is it called?","o":["Synovial sarcoma","Synovioma","Malignant synovioma","Synovial carcinoma","Malignant mesothelioma"],"e":"Synovium gives synovioma (benign) and synovial sarcoma (malignant). Malignant mesenchymal tumours are sarcomas. Only mesothelium uses \"benign\" or \"malignant\" before a shared name (mesothelioma).","w":["Synovioma is the benign tumour.","The malignant form is named synovial sarcoma, not malignant synovioma.","Carcinomas are epithelial, and synovium is mesenchymal.","Mesothelioma arises from mesothelium."],"k":"Synovium: synovioma / synovial sarcoma.","rn":[["","What are malignant mesenchymal tumours called?","What are malignant mesenchymal tumours called?","Sarcomas"]]},{"id":47121,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A 62-year-old man has a colonoscopy for a change in bowel habit. A lesion is found that has formed a bump on the surface, but the surface over it is ulcerated and bleeding. Biopsy shows cells digging down below the lining in finger-like tongues, many abnormal mitoses, and a patchy cut surface with grey areas. Blood tests show he is anaemic.<br><br>What is the most likely classification of this lesion, and what best explains the grey areas?","o":["Malignant; necrosis from inadequate vascular perfusion","Benign; calcification of a long-standing polyp","Borderline; necrosis from immune attack on the tumour","Malignant; areas of normal tissue trapped in the tumour","Benign; haemorrhage into a pedunculated stalk"],"e":"Step 1: endophytic growth with tongues of tissue invading below the surface, ulceration and many abnormal mitoses make this malignant; malignant tumours may form a protrusion early but eventually invade. Step 2: the grey areas of a heterogeneous cut surface are necrosis. Malignant tumours grow so fast that the middle does not get enough new blood vessels, so cells die from lack of oxygen and nutrients. The anaemia fits blood loss from the ulcerated surface.","w":["Invasion and ulceration exclude a benign polyp.","Borderline tumours lack invasion, and necrosis here is from poor perfusion.","The grey areas are dead tissue, not normal tissue.","Invasion and ulceration exclude a benign tumour."],"k":"Invasion + ulceration = malignant; grey core = perfusion necrosis.","rn":[["","Why do malignant tumours show central necrosis?","Why do malignant tumours show central necrosis?","Inadequate vascular perfusion"]]},{"id":47122,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A 51-year-old woman has a routine mammogram showing an area of calcification. A biopsy shows breast ducts expanded and packed with cancer cells, with a calcified necrotic centre. The basement membrane and myoepithelial layer surround the cells completely. Six months later, a repeat biopsy of a new lump shows the same cells in the surrounding stroma.<br><br>How has the diagnosis changed between the two biopsies, and why does it matter?","o":["From DCIS to invasive ductal carcinoma; the treatment is different","From a benign adenoma to DCIS; the treatment stays identical for both lesions","From DCIS to a borderline tumour; no treatment is needed","From invasive carcinoma to DCIS; the prognosis improves","From DCIS to a sarcoma; the tissue of origin has changed"],"e":"Step 1: the first biopsy shows cancer cells filling the ducts with calcified central necrosis (seen on mammography) and an intact basement membrane: ductal carcinoma in situ. Step 2: cells in the surrounding stroma mean they have broken through the basement membrane, which makes it invasive ductal carcinoma (it started in the ducts but is now invading). The treatments are different, so the pathologist's call between in situ and invasive is critical.","w":["The first biopsy already showed cancer cells, and the treatments differ.","Borderline tumours lack invasion, which is now present.","The change is from in situ to invasive, not the other way round.","The cells still come from the duct epithelium, so it is still a carcinoma."],"k":"DCIS breaching the basement membrane = invasive ductal carcinoma.","rn":[["","What is DCIS called once it breaks through?","What is DCIS called once it breaks through?","Invasive ductal carcinoma"],["","DCIS","DCIS","Ductal carcinoma in situ"],["","Image card: What breast cancer is this? What are A and B?","What breast cancer is this? What are A and B?","Ductal carcinoma in situ; A: calcified central necrosis; B: intact basement membrane"]]},{"id":47123,"t":"Benign Tumours","s":"A 34-year-old woman presents with weight loss, a fast heart rate and heat intolerance. Thyroid function tests show excess thyroid hormone. A small, well-circumscribed thyroid nodule is removed; histology shows encapsulated cells closely resembling normal thyroid follicles with rare mitoses and no invasion.<br><br>What is the tumour, and how does it cause her symptoms?","o":["Thyroid adenoma causing thyrotoxicosis by hormone production","Thyroid adenocarcinoma causing weight loss by paraneoplastic effects","Thyroid adenoma causing symptoms by pressure on adjacent tissue","Borderline thyroid tumour causing symptoms by duct obstruction","Thyroid papilloma causing symptoms by blood loss from ulceration"],"e":"Step 1: encapsulation, good resemblance to normal tissue, rare mitoses and no invasion are benign features; a benign tumour of glandular (secretory) epithelium is an adenoma, e.g. thyroid adenoma. Step 2: benign tumours can still cause problems, including production of a hormone; the example is a benign thyroid tumour causing thyrotoxicosis (too much thyroid hormone).","w":["There is no invasion, so it is not a carcinoma.","It is small and her symptoms match excess hormone, not pressure.","It shows no malignant features, and obstruction is not the mechanism.","Thyroid is glandular, so the benign tumour is an adenoma, and it is not ulcerated."],"k":"Benign thyroid tumour → thyrotoxicosis via hormone production.","rn":[["","Benign thyroid tumour producing hormone: effect?","Benign thyroid tumour producing hormone: effect?","Thyrotoxicosis"]]},{"id":47124,"t":"Histogenetic Classification","s":"A 17-year-old has pain and swelling near the knee. Imaging shows a destructive lesion in the bone. A biopsy shows malignant cells that are making bone, invading the surrounding tissue. A second patient, aged 50, has a slowly growing, soft lump under the skin of the back; it is removed and shows benign fat cells.<br><br>What are the correct names for these two tumours?","o":["Osteosarcoma and lipoma","Osteoma and liposarcoma","Osteocarcinoma and lipoma","Osteosarcoma and liposarcoma","Chondrosarcoma and lipoma"],"e":"Step 1: bone is a mesenchymal (connective) tissue, so a malignant bone tumour is a sarcoma: osteosarcoma. Step 2: benign mesenchymal tumours are named with a prefix for the cell of origin plus -oma, and adipose tissue (fat) gives lipoma. The malignant fat tumour would be liposarcoma, and the benign bone tumour would be osteoma.","w":["The bone tumour is malignant and the fat tumour benign, so these are reversed.","Carcinomas are epithelial; malignant bone tumours are sarcomas.","The fat tumour is benign, so it is a lipoma.","Chondro- is cartilage; this tumour is making bone."],"k":"Mesenchymal: benign -oma; malignant -sarcoma."},{"id":47125,"t":"Histogenic Classification of Tumours","s":"A 58-year-old woman has a breast cancer removed. The pathologist reports a medium-sized primary tumour. Several of the lymph nodes removed contain cancer cells. Staging scans of the lungs and liver show no deposits.<br><br>What is her TNM stage, and what does the N result suggest about the tumour's behaviour?","o":["T2N1M0; cells have broken off and travelled through the lymphatics","T2N0M1; cells have spread through the bloodstream to the lungs","T1N0M0; the tumour is confined to its tissue of origin","T2N1M0; the tumour is benign and has not invaded","T4N2M2; the tumour has spread to two distant organs"],"e":"Step 1: a medium-sized tumour is T2, some lymph node involvement is N1, and no deposits in lungs or liver is M0, so T2N1M0. Step 2: lymph node involvement suggests metastasis; some cells have broken off and are travelling through the lymphatic system. This changes treatment, as you want N0.","w":["The nodes are involved and the scans are clear, so N1 and M0.","The nodes contain cancer, so it is not confined.","Spread to nodes means it is malignant and has invaded.","The tumour is medium-sized and there are no distant deposits."],"k":"Node involvement = lymphatic spread; T2N1M0.","rn":[["","Major Categories of Origin of Tumours","Major Categories of Origin of Tumours","epithelial cells; connective/mesenchymal tissues; haematopoietic system; lymphatic system"]]},{"id":47126,"t":"Tumour Staging","s":"A 66-year-old man has a colorectal cancer resected. Histology shows the tumour has grown through the muscularis propria layer of the bowel wall. Twelve lymph nodes are examined and none contain tumour. Staging imaging shows no spread elsewhere.<br><br>What is his Dukes stage, and what is the five-year survival shown for it?","o":["Dukes B; 70%","Dukes A; 90+%","Dukes C; 35%","Dukes D; not shown","Dukes B; 35%"],"e":"Step 1: Dukes A is confined to the submucosa; B has spread through the muscularis propria but does not yet involve the lymph nodes (e.g. pT3N0M0); C involves at least one node; D has spread to another part of the body. This tumour is through the muscularis propria with clear nodes and no distant spread, so Dukes B. Step 2: the figure shows five-year survival of 90+% for A, 70% for B and 35% for C.","w":["It has gone beyond the submucosa.","No lymph nodes are involved.","There is no distant spread.","Dukes B survival is 70%; 35% is stage C."],"k":"Through muscularis propria, nodes clear = Dukes B (70%).","rn":[["","Dukes B: extent?","Dukes B: extent?","Through muscularis propria; No lymph nodes"]]},{"id":47127,"t":"Tumour Grading","s":"Two patients have adenocarcinoma of the colon at the same stage. In patient X the tumour still forms glands similar to normal mucosa, with few mitoses. In patient Y the tumour grows as solid sheets with little gland formation, many abnormal nuclei and a high mitotic index.<br><br>Which statement best compares the two tumours?","o":["X is low grade and Y high grade; Y is likely more aggressive","X is high grade and Y low grade; X is likely more aggressive","Both are the same grade because they share the same stage","X is benign and Y is malignant, because only Y has mitoses","Y is a sarcoma because it no longer forms glands"],"e":"Step 1: grading describes how closely the tumour resembles its tissue of origin. X keeps glands (well differentiated, probably grade 1) and Y has a solid pattern with little gland formation and a high mitotic index (poorly differentiated, probably grade 3). Step 2: grading correlates strongly with survival; a tumour that doesn't look like its tissue of origin is probably more aggressive and is treated differently. Grade and stage are separate.","w":["This reverses the grades.","Grade and stage are separate measures.","Both are adenocarcinomas, so both are malignant.","Both arise from glandular epithelium; loss of glands reflects grade."],"k":"Same stage, different grade → different aggressiveness.","rn":[["","What 2 description-based grading scales exist?","What 2 description-based grading scales exist?","Low/medium/high grade; Well/moderately/poorly differentiated"],["","Grade 3","Grade 3","High grade, poorly differentiated"],["","Grade 1","Grade 1","Low grade, well differentiated"]]},{"id":47128,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A 39-year-old woman has an ovarian mass removed. The pathologist notes irregular architecture, nuclei stacked in layers, variation in nuclear size and quite high mitotic activity. Extensive sampling shows no cells breaking into the surrounding tissue.<br><br>How should this tumour be classified, and what does this mean for her management?","o":["Borderline; treated differently from benign and malignant tumours","Malignant; treated in exactly the same way as an invasive carcinoma","Benign; no follow-up is ever needed","Malignant; staged as Dukes C","Benign; it is an adenoma that will never change"],"e":"Step 1: irregular architecture, nuclear stratification, pleomorphism and mitotic activity are malignant features, but the most important criterion, invasion, is absent: this is a borderline tumour, and borderline tumours of the ovary are quite common. Step 2: borderline tumours have intermediate behaviour and are treated in a specific way, differently from benign and from malignant tumours.","w":["Without invasion it does not meet the malignant criterion.","The malignant-looking features go beyond benign.","Dukes staging is for colorectal cancer, and this is not invasive.","The features are not those of a simple benign adenoma."],"k":"Malignant features without invasion = borderline, managed specifically.","rn":[["","Are borderline tumours treated like benign or malignant?","Are borderline tumours treated like benign or malignant?","Neither; Treated in a specific way"],["","Which 4 malignant features do borderline tumours show?","Which 4 malignant features do borderline tumours show?","Irregular architecture; Nuclear stratification; Pleomorphism; Mitotic activity"]]},{"id":47129,"t":"Malignant Tumours","s":"A 68-year-old long-term smoker has a lung tumour. He develops features of excess ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone), along with marked weight loss and weakness. Scans also show deposits in the liver. The tumour is not obstructing his airways.<br><br>Which combination of problems of malignant tumours does he show?","o":["Hormone production, paraneoplastic debility and metastases","Pressure, obstruction and transformation","Blood loss, obstruction and anxiety","Hormone production, obstruction and blood loss","Pressure, transformation and paraneoplastic debility"],"e":"Step 1: some lung tumours produce hormones such as ACTH and vasopressin (ADH). Step 2: weight loss and debility are other paraneoplastic effects (running alongside the cancer, as metabolism switches while the body tries to deal with it), and liver deposits are secondary tumours (metastases). The airways are not obstructed, and transformation is a benign-tumour problem.","w":["There is no obstruction, and transformation applies to benign tumours.","There is no bleeding or obstruction described.","There is no obstruction or blood loss described.","Transformation is a benign-tumour problem."],"k":"Lung tumour: ACTH/ADH, paraneoplastic effects, metastases.","rn":[["","What 3 other problems do malignant tumours cause?","What 3 other problems do malignant tumours cause?","Hormone production; Paraneoplastic effects; Anxiety and pain"],["","What 2 paraneoplastic effects are listed?","What 2 paraneoplastic effects are listed?","Weight loss; Debility"]]},{"id":47130,"t":"Classifications Now and Future","s":"At an MDT (multidisciplinary team) meeting, a patient is presented with a liver deposit of adenocarcinoma. Imaging and endoscopy find no primary tumour anywhere. The pathologist can grade the deposit but cannot say where it originated. The team is uncertain about treatment.<br><br>What is the key gap, and why does it make treatment difficult?","o":["The histogenesis is unknown, which classification needs to guide treatment","The grade is unknown, which is needed to name the tumour","The behaviour is unknown, so it may be a benign tumour","The stage is unknown, because grade cannot be measured on a deposit","The tumour is borderline, so no treatment can be chosen"],"e":"Step 1: the deposit is assumed to be a metastasis of a cancer whose site of origin cannot be found (an unknown primary); its histogenesis, the specific cell or tissue of origin, is missing. Step 2: accurate classification by behaviour and histogenesis is essential for diagnosis, prognosis and treatment, so these patients are really hard to treat. The original tumour may have been very small and broken off very early.","w":["The deposit can be graded; it is the origin that is missing.","A metastatic adenocarcinoma is malignant.","Grade can be measured; the problem is the unknown origin.","A metastasis is malignant, not borderline."],"k":"Unknown primary = unknown histogenesis = hard to treat.","rn":[["","Why is an unknown primary site hard to treat?","Why is an unknown primary site hard to treat?","Origin unknown, only metastasis found"]]}]);
